one\" confidence, optional), and (e) effect-size heterogeneity across task tiers.\n\nConditions (cells). Factor 1: Reasoning style — within-subject across every task: C0 No-CoT (direct action emission, control); C1 Zero-shot CoT (\"Let's think step by step\" before action); C2 Few-shot CoT (k=3–5 worked thinking/action/observation exemplars drawn from the same tier, disjoint from the test set); C3 Self-consistency (C1 sampled at temperature 0.7 with N=5, majority-vote on the emitted action); optional C4 Tool-augmented CoT (explicit scratchpad sub-tool such as `plan` / `verify` between thought and action with matched token budget to C1). Factor 2 (blocking): WorkArena++ tier — list/lookup, search/navigation, forms, configuration, plus any composite \"plan\" tier (provisional; unverified). Tasks are stratified by tier to support within-tier paired analysis.\n\nPlatform. WorkArena++ (ServiceNow atomic + composite tasks, served via BrowserGym). One ServiceNow instance per worker, deterministic reset between trials. Task selection: run all tasks of each selected tier; otherwise a stratified random sample of N tasks per tier with tier share proportional to population. Because the exact tier names and task IDs are NOT verified from the workspace, the design treats the tier list as a parameter to be filled in by the researcher.\n\nAgent stack. Base LLM: a single frontier chat model (researcher's choice; default proposed = a current mid-size model such as GPT-4-class / Claude-3-class) with deterministic decoding: temperature 0 for C0/C1/C2, 0.7 for C3, top-p 1, fixed seed. Same model identity across all conditions. Browser driver: BrowserGym over headless Chromium controlling a ServiceNow instance. Observation → prompt: HTML ax-tree (or DOM-pruned) snippet + last action + last error + current goal string + chat history of (thought, action, observation) tuples. Action format = BrowserGym's discrete action enum. CoT is inserted immediately before the action token, in a dedicated `<think>...</think>` channel that is also logged for later failure coding; length-controlled by an instruction \"Use ≤ N tokens of reasoning, N≈120\"; cost-budget matched across conditions where possible. Prompt template: one shared system prompt; only the reasoning-instruction block differs between C0 and C1/C2/C3.\n\nProtocol. Trials: 3 independent trials per (task × condition) to absorb stochasticity; median used for paired tests. Max steps: 25 per task (or tier-specific cap); wall-clock timeout 10 min/trial. Retry policy: no retries inside a trial; an infrastructure error triggers at most one rerun and is flagged. Seeding: Python/NumPy seeds, BrowserGym instance snapshot ID, ServiceNow user fixed. State reset: hard rollback to a known post-login ServiceNow state before every trial; verified by a sentinel page load. Randomization: Latin-square ordering of conditions across tasks; exemplars in C2 shuffled per trial from a fixed pool of k.\n\nControls & confounders. Prompt-length and token cost: C0 receives a filler \"state your plan in 1 short sentence then act\" instruction of comparable verbosity so prompt length is not a free parameter. Exemplar shuffle and exemplar-pool identity held fixed. Identical temperature across deterministic conditions (C0/C1/C2). Tool access identical (same BrowserGym action set; no extra retrievers). HTTP caching disabled for the agent's calls; enabled for static prompt scaffolding to control cost. Antitampering: a held-out \"canary\" task in which the No-CoT prompt is patched to include the literal CoT phrase — must NOT change its behaviour, validating that gains in C1–C3 are not instruction artefacts leaking. Order effects: counterbalanced with Latin square; \"first-trial contamination\" analysed as a covariate.\n\nMetrics beyond success rate. Step efficiency = steps / optimal-steps (where an upper bound is known from task definitions). Tokens = input + output per trial; success per 1k tokens; $ per success. Latency = wall-clock seconds per trial and per step. Reasoning-error taxonomy (manual coding of failed trials): wrong goal interpretation; hallucinated UI element; action-format/parse error; loop/repeat of identical (state, action); premature stop; incorrect post-condition check. Calibration: Brier score on self-reported \"done\" confidence (only if the prompt asks for it, with identical instruction across conditions).\n\nAnalysis plan. Primary tests: paired (task-level) two-sided test of success between C1 and C0, C2 vs. C0, and C3 vs. C0 (subject-level pairing because every task sees every condition). Correction: Holm–Bonferroni across the 3 primary CoT contrasts. Effect sizes: Cohen's d for paired proportions + bootstrap 95% CI on Δ; same for cost-adjusted score. Stratified breakdown: by tier (interaction test); pre-registered \"where does CoT help / hurt\" decomposition. Ablations: drop scratchpad (C1 vs. C4); few-shot count k ∈ {1, 3, 5}; prompt-length-matched sub-analysis inserting equivalent filler in place of CoT. Robustness: rerun top 10% of tasks from each tier with seed variation to estimate variance. All analyses are registered in advance of execution against this design.\n\nFeasibility check. No execution is attempted here. Risks to feasibility when run: API cost (mitigated by ≤ N tasks/tier and 3 trials; C3 self-consistency is ≈5× the C1 token bill), BrowserGym instance provisioning (requires a ServiceNow PDI or shared testing tenant), and evaluation wall-clock (mitigated by parallelisation across the available CPU cores).\n\n## 3) ASSUMPTIONS & UNCERTAINTIES\n\nAssumptions:\n- A1 WorkArena++ is interpreted as the BrowserGym/ServiceNow benchmark with broad tiers (list/lookup, search/navigation, forms, configuration, plus composite \"plan\" tasks). Exact tier names and task counts are NOT verified and must be confirmed by the researcher.\n- A2 \"Improve\" is read primarily as paired task-level success-rate Δ and secondarily as cost-adjusted success per 1k tokens; both are 