{"id": "ce378cde-0535-4b12-9bff-9f8cf38e5491", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T15:39:46.782448", "source": "user", "parent_id": "68f62828-bd3a-4dff-b3b8-7bd707f22dfe", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Approach approved. Resolutions to your two flagged uncertainties, which you should bake into the full design rather than re-litigate: (1) PRIMARY ENDPOINT = paired task-level success-rate Δ (CoT − No-CoT) as the headline; cost-adjusted success per 1k tokens is a CO-PRIMARY reported alongside (same analysis battery, same Holm–Bonferroni family), not a quality-gate — i.e. we do not require both to agree to call CoT 'improving', but we report both and pre-specify how a disagreement would be narrated (effect-size heterogeneity by tier + a dominance-plot). (2) SCOPE OF CoT CONDITIONS = the CANONICAL headline contrast is C1 (Zero-shot 'think step by step' immediately before action) vs. C0 (matched-verbosity filler, no reasoning); C2 (Few-shot CoT, k=3) and C3 (Self-consistency, N=5, T=0.7, majority action) are SECONDARY cells in the same factorial; C4 (tool-augmented scratchpad) is OPTIONAL and only included if you can credibly length-match it to C1 — if not, drop it rather than half-spec it. Keep a single fixed base model across all cells (your choice, document it), and keep the <think>…</think> channel logged for failure coding. Now produce the FULL design document (single self-contained write-up) with these sections in this order, each substantive, no filler: A. Restated research question and operational hypotheses (H1 for C1 vs. C0 on success rate; H1c for cost-adjusted; H2 for C2 vs. C1 ablation; H3 for C3 vs. C1) with a one-line pre-registered decision rule for each; B. Platform — WorkArena++ tier taxonomy you are adopting (state explicitly that the exact tier list is ratified-by-researcher and provide the candidate list you will use; do not invent task IDs you have not verified); C. Agent stack — LLM, BrowserGym version assumption, observation/action interface, full prompt template skeleton showing the exact slot that differs between C0 and C1 (and what changes for C2/C3), length-control mechanism, decoding settings; D. Protocol — trials per cell, max steps, timeouts, reset/sentinel procedure, randomization/counterbalancing, exemplar-pool management for C2 with explicit disjoint-from-test guarantee; E. Metrics — primary, co-primary, secondary, with operational definitions and units (success, steps, tokens in/out, $/success, latency, failure-mode codes with the 6-category taxonomy you outlined, Brier for calibration conditioned on identical 'done-confidence' instruction across cells); F. Controls & confounders — the matched-verbosity filler rule, the CoT-leak canary task and how you will read it, tool/access parity, caching rules, prompt-version pinning, temperature parity for deterministic cells; G. Analysis plan — statistical unit (task), pairing, primary test family (Wilcoxon signed-rank for proportions plus McNemar/Binomial as a robustness tiebreaker), Holm–Bonferroni across the 3 planned CoT contrasts, bootstrap 95% CIs on Δ, effect size Cohen's d for paired proportions + Cliff's δ as non-parametric backup, stratified breakdown by tier with a tier×condition interaction test, ablations enumerated (k∈{1,3,5}; dropping scratchpad C4; prompt-length-matched sub-analysis), robustness replications on a 10% subsample with fresh seeds; H. Power & sample-size — give a SAMPLE-SIZE PHILOSOPHY paragraph (functional rather than aspirational, since no execution) showing the paired-proportion power calculation for detecting say a 5–10 pp Δ at α=0.05, power 0.8, with Holm correction, and translate that into per-tier allocation given the unverified tier counts (be explicit that this is a planning sketch contingent on confirming tier sizes); I. Threats to validity — list construct, internal, external, and statistical-conclusion threats separately, each with the mitigation you built into the design (not a separate paragraph that didn't make it into the plan); J. Feasibility & compute — token/$ order-of-magnitude estimate per cell given N tasks × 3 trials × max-steps × prompt size, ServiceNow/BrowserGym provisioning assumption, parallelization across the available 24 CPU cores, time budget; K. Pre-registration checklist — fixed-vs-explored items, what is locked before any execution, what is exploratory and labelled as such; L. Limitations — single-model, single-template, English-only ServiceNow locale, evaluator LLM bias if you use one for failure coding, absence of human-in-the-loop, that the design measures task-solving ability not generalization to unseen ServiceNow tenants. Hard constraints for the final document: (i) it must read as a complete, executable design that a third party could pick up and run; (ii) it must not claim the experiment was run, must not cite specific numeric baselines from prior WorkArena++ papers you have not verified, must not invent task IDs or model version numbers; (iii) flag every number that depends on an unverified assumption in line with 'assumption X / pending confirmation'; (iv) keep the write-up tight — prefer tables for condition matrix, metrics table, analysis matrix; (v) end with a one-paragraph 'summary for the reviewer' that answers the original question in terms of what the experiment WOULD show, not what it does show. Proceed.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 18}
{"id": "df6c7f65-36b7-4d25-b285-acfe0050498a", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T15:41:33.370865", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "ce378cde-0535-4b12-9bff-9f8cf38e5491", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-408116b6-0c9b-4a93-bf9e-000d74e7a75f", "tool_call": {"id": "call-408116b6-0c9b-4a93-bf9e-000d74e7a75f", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Full design result with 12 sections + reviewer summary\",\"stage\":\"result\",\"answer\":\"# Experiment Design — Does Chain-of-Thought (CoT) Prompting Improve LLM-Agent Task-Solving in WorkArena++?\\n\\nThis is a design-only deliverable. Nothing has been executed and no measurements are reported. Every concrete number that depends on an unverified assumption is flagged inline as *[assumption X / pending confirmation]*.\\n\\n## A. Research Question and Operational Hypotheses\\n\\n**Research question (operational).** Within a fixed agent stack and a fixed base LLM, does inserting explicit chain-of-thought (CoT) reasoning immediately before action emission improve task-solving on WorkArena++?\\n\\n| # | Hypothesis | Test direction | One-line pre-registered decision rule |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| H1 (primary, headline) | CoT (C1) > No-CoT (C0) on paired task-level success rate. | one-sided, Δ > 0 | Declare \\\"C1 improves over C0\\\" iff (i) Wilcoxon signed-rank p ≤ α after Holm–Bonferroni, (ii) paired Δ > 0, and (iii) its 95% bootstrap CI excludes 0 with at least 80% of resampled mass above 0. |\\n| H1c (co-primary) | C1 > C0 on cost-adjusted success (success per 1k total tokens). | one-sided, Δ > 0 | Same template as H1, applied to the second metric in the same Holm family. Two co-primaries are reported; a disagreement is narrated by tier-stratified Δ and a dominance plot, not by gating them. |\\n| H2 (secondary) | C2 (Few-shot CoT, k=3) differs from C1. | two-sided | Report sign, magnitude, and Holm-adjusted p; the narrative question is whether few-shot exemplars add, subtract, or match zero-shot CoT. |\\n| H3 (secondary) | C3 (Self-consistency, N=5, T=0.7) differs from C1. | two-sided | Report sign, magnitude, and Holm-adjusted p; the narrative question is whether the 5× token spend yields a measurable success gain over C1. |\\n\\nHolm family for primary inference = {H1, H1c, H2, H3}. A pre-registered dominance plot (Δ success on x-axis, Δ cost-adjusted score on y-axis, one point per tier) is the canonical visual for telling the success-vs-cost story.\\n\\n## B. Platform — WorkArena++ Tier Taxonomy\\n\\nWorkArena++ is assumed to be the BrowserGym-served ServiceNow benchmark. The exact tier list is **ratified-by-researcher / pending confirmation** — no specific task IDs from the suite are used in this design.\\n\\n**Adopted candidate tier list** (the design treats these as parameters to be locked pre-execution):\\n\\n| Tier code | Candidate name | Example capability | Sample-size stratum |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| T1 | list / lookup | read-only retrieval on ServiceNow records | small-to-mid |\\n| T2 | search / navigation | multi-page traversal, filter/sort | mid |\\n| T3 | forms | create / edit record via form fields | mid |\\n| T4 | configuration | change system settings / workflows | small |\\n| T5 | composite / plan | multi-step, cross-page objectives | small (hardest) |\\n\\nIf the ratified list differs, the only knob that must change is per-tier sample allocation in §H; everything downstream is tier-symbolic. Counters external to the design (composite \\\"plan\\\" tasks) are revisited once the ratified tier set is in hand.\\n\\n## C. Agent Stack\\n\\n**Base LLM.** Single fixed chat model across all cells. *Default proposal — pending confirmation of API access and pinning*: a current frontier chat model in the GPT-4o / Claude-3.5-Sonnet class with ≥128k context, function- or tool-calling disabled (WorkArena++ uses a discrete action enum, not tool calls). The exact model id is **pinned in a single `model.lock` file** before execution and never changed mid-run. All four cells C0/C1/C2/C3 use that same id.\\n\\n**BrowserGym / ServiceNow.** BrowserGym (release: *assumption — use the most recent stable BrowserGym that ships with the adopted WorkArena++* / pending confirmation) over headless Chromium controlling a ServiceNow Personal Developer Instance (PDI) or shared testing tenant. Assumption: one instance per worker; instance snapshot id is fixed. UI locale: en-US (see §L).\\n\\n**Observation → prompt interface.**\\n- Page observation = pruned HTML accessibility tree (DOM-pruned to interactive and labelled elements), capped at ~6k tokens.\\n- Action format = BrowserGym's discrete enum (e.g., `click(element_id)`, `type(element_id, text)`, `select(...)`, `navigate(url)`, `done()`).\\n- Prompt payload per step = `{system, goal, observation_tree, last_action, last_error, chat_history[(thought, action, observation)]}`. History is windowed to the last K turns (K = 10 default) to control prompt size.\\n\\n**CoT insertion slot.** A single `<think>…</think>` block immediately before the action, channel-separated and **logged verbatim** for failure-mode coding (§E).\\n\\n**Length-control mechanism.** All four cells are constrained to a per-step output budget of ≤ **192 tokens total** (think + action combined), enforced by a stop sequence and a downstream parser. This makes CoT-length an *independent variable measured, not a confound allowed to drift*. The §G ablation explicitly tests what happens when this cap is doubled.\\n\\n**Decoding settings.**\\n\\n| Cell | Temperature | Top-p | # samples | Aggregation | Seed |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| C0 No-CoT | 0 | 1.0 | 1 | n/a | fixed |\\n| C1 Zero-shot CoT | 0 | 1.0 | 1 | n/a | fixed |\\n| C2 Few-shot CoT | 0 | 1.0 | 1 | n/a | fixed |\\n| C3 Self-consistency | 0.7 | 1.0 | 5 | majority action (think discarded) | fixed, plus per-sample index |\\n\\n**Prompt template skeleton (literal slots).** Slots `<R>` and `<F>` are the **only** differences across cells; everything else is identical.\\n\\n```\\nSYSTEM:\\nYou are an agent controlling a ServiceNow instance. You observe an accessibility\\ntree, you think if asked, and you emit exactly one BrowserGym action. Never exceed\\n192 output tokens.\\n\\nGOAL:\\n{goal}\\n\\nOBSERVATION:\\n{ax_tree}\\n\\nLAST_ACTION: {last_action}\\nLAST_ERROR:  {last_error}\\n\\n<R>  ← reasoning-instruction block (differs per cell; see below)\\n<F>  ← exemplar block (only present in C2)\\n\\nNOW EMIT ONE ACTION.\\n```\\n\\n`R` per cell:\\n\\n| Cell | `<R>` block (literal) |\\n|---|---|\\n| C0 | \\\"Plan in one short sentence, then act. (This is a no-reasoning filler to match verbosity.)\\\" |\\n| C1 | \\\"Think step by step about the goal and the current page before acting.\\\" |\\n| C2 | inherits C1's `<R>`, exact same text. |\\n| C3 | inherits C1's `<R>`, exact same text; only temperature / N change. |\\n\\n`F` per cell:\\n\\n| Cell | `<F>` block |\\n|---|---|\\n| C0 | n/a |\\n| C1 | n/a |\\n| C2 | k=3 worked (think, action, observation) exemplars drawn from the same tier, **disjoint from the test set** (see §D) |\\n| C3 | n/a (no exemplars; reasoning sampled 5×) |\\n\\nC4 (tool-augmented scratchpad) is included **only if** its `<R>` can be length-matched to C1 within ±10% of output-token expectation on a 50-step dry run; otherwise it is dropped from the run. The design is built so C4 can be added or removed without touching anything else.\\n\\n## D. Protocol\\n\\n| Knob | Value | Rationale |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Trials per (task × cell) | 3 | absorbs stochasticity for paired tests; 3 is the budget–quality trade-off (see §H). |\\n| Median of 3 trials used as the task's score | yes | reduces variance without inflating matched-pairs design |\\n| Max steps / trial | 25 (T5: 40) | tier-specific cap; user can revise |\\n| Wall-clock timeout / trial | 10 min | aborts hung agents |\\n| Per-step HTTP timeout | 30 s | protects against infinite loads |\\n| Retry policy inside a trial | none | a step retry would break paired integrity |\\n| Trial rerun on infra error | at most 1, flagged | distinguishes LLM-side from infra-side failures |\\n| Environment reset | hard rollback to a known post-login snapshot before every trial |\\n| Reset sentinel | assert that `[data-test-sentinel=\\\"ready\\\"]` element is present after reset; otherwise abort trial |\\n| Randomization | Latin-square ordering of cells across tasks | controls first-trial contamination |\\n| Exemplar pool (C2) | 5 held-out exemplar tasks per tier, disjoint from test tasks by construction; pool is fixed and published | see exemplar-management rule below |\\n| Exemplar sampling per trial | each trial draws k=3 without replacement from the tier pool; draw is randomized per trial but pool is fixed | prevents within-trial overfitting while preserving matched-pairs |\\n| Seed hygiene | Python, NumPy, the LLM SDK, and BrowserGym are all seeded; seeds are logged per trial | reproducibility |\\n\\n**Exemplar-pool disjointness rule (C2).** The exemplar pool is constructed once, before any test task is sampled, by selecting k_pool=5 demonstration tasks per tier from the WorkArena++ training portion or — if the suite does not ship a train/test split — from a frozen-enough carve-out. The test task set is then drawn **strictly disjoint** from this pool by id. A single-bit audit (`exemplar_in_test_set → run aborts`) is in CI before any result is reported. This guarantees C2 cannot leak task content into the test set.\\n\\n## E. Metrics\\n\\n| Metric | Tier | Definition | Unit |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| **success** | primary (H1) | Indicator that the trial reached the task's terminal `done()` with the success predicate true on the post-condition check. Per trial; median of 3 per (task × cell). | 0/1 |\\n| **Δ_success** | primary | Paired (task-level) C1 − C0 on the median-success-per-task summary. | proportion points |\\n| **cost-adjusted score** | co-primary (H1c) | success per 1,000 total tokens (input + output, summed over the whole trial). | 1 / 1k tok |\\n| **Δ_cost-adjusted** | co-primary | Paired C1 − C0 on cost-adjusted score. | 1 / 1k tok |\\n| **steps** | secondary | Median steps-to-success over the 3 trials; for failed trials = max_steps + 1 (censored). | int |\\n| **tokens_in** | secondary | Sum of prompt tokens across all turns in a trial. | tokens |\\n| **tokens_out** | secondary | Sum of completion tokens across all turns in a trial. | tokens |\\n| **$/success** | secondary | (tokens_in × $in + tokens_out × $out) / success, where $/Mtok are the model's published rates. | USD |\\n| **latency** | secondary | Wall-clock seconds per trial and per step. | s |\\n| **hallucination rate** | secondary | Per-trial indicator that any step's `<think>` references a UI element not in the ax-tree (regex/linter rule on element ids). | 0/1 |\\n| **loop rate** | secondary | Per-trial indicator of ≥ 2 consecutive identical (state, action) pairs. | 0/1 |\\n| **failure-mode code** | secondary | One of 6 codes, labelled on every failed trial (§taxonomy below). | categorical |\\n| **Brier on done-confidence** | secondary | Optional. The agent emits a 0–1 \\\"done-confidence\\\" before each `done()` action; Brier = mean((confidence − actual_success)²). The instruction to emit this confidence is identical across cells. | score |\\n\\n**Failure-mode taxonomy (6 codes).** A failed trial is assigned exactly one code by manual review, with the `<think>` log as primary evidence:\\n1. **wrong_goal** — agent misinterpreted the instruction.\\n2. **hallucinated_element** — agent referenced a UI element not in the ax-tree.\\n3. **action_format_error** — agent emitted a malformed action.\\n4. **loop** — agent repeated the same (state, action).\\n5. **premature_stop** — agent called `done()` before goal was actually achieved.\\n6. **post_condition_check_failed** — agent thought it was done but the predicate failed.\\n\\nInter-rater reliability is reported for the subset of failures double-coded by a second human; agreement target is Cohen's κ ≥ 0.7. If a judge LLM is used as the second coder (allowed only at this auditing step, not as the primary coder), the prompt is fixed and its outputs are reported alongside κ to keep evaluator bias visible.\\n\\n## F. Controls and Confounders\\n\\n| Confounder | Control rule in the design |\\n|---|---|\\n| **Prompt length as free parameter** | C0 is given a matched-verbosity filler (\\\"Plan in one short sentence, then act.\\\"). Output cap (192 tok) is identical across all cells. A length-matched sub-analysis in §G explicitly swaps CoT for filler to test whether any effect survives. |\\n| **CoT leakage into C0 (instruction artefact)** | **Canary task**: 10 atomically-short look-up tasks are run with C0's prompt **but with C1's literal `<R>` block pasted in verbatim**. If the canary shows >2 pp drift vs. plain C0, the architecture leaks and the design quarantines those 10 tasks out of H1 inference. |\\n| **Temperature drift between deterministic cells** | C0, C1, C2 all run at T=0; only C3 switches to T=0.7. The cost of C3 is recorded separately and not silently averaged into the headline. |\\n| **Tool / capability parity** | All cells use the same BrowserGym action enum; function-calling, retrieval, and web search are disabled across the board. |\\n| **Caching** | LLM call-side caching (e.g., response cache) is **disabled**; static prompt-template caching on the client side is enabled. Each trial's exact tokens are re-counted in post-processing. |\\n| **Prompt-version pinning** | Every prompt template is stored under `prompts/v{date}/{cell}.txt`. The execution harness refuses to start if the date stamp on the loaded prompt ≠ the one in `model.lock`. No mid-run edits are permitted. |\\n| **Exemplar-pool identity** | Frozen before any test task is sampled (see §D). |\\n| **Order effects** | Latin square across cells within each task. The first-trial contamination effect is tested as a covariate in §G. |\\n| **Random seed reproducibility** | All RNGs seeded and logged per trial; failure to reproduce a trial from logs is itself a logged event. |\\n\\n## G. Analysis Plan\\n\\n**Statistical unit.** The task. One observation per (task × cell) is the median of 3 trials.\\n\\n**Pairing.** Every task sees every cell (within-subject). Pairing is therefore at the task level.\\n\\n**Primary test family.**\\n\\n| Hypothesis | Primary test | Robustness tiebreaker |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| H1 (success Δ) | Wilcoxon signed-rank, one-sided C1 > C0 | McNemar on per-trial success counts (paired binomial) |\\n| H1c (cost-adjusted Δ) | Wilcoxon signed-rank, one-sided C1 > C0 | paired bootstrap on the score |\\n| H2 (C2 vs. C1) | Wilcoxon signed-rank, two-sided | paired bootstrap |\\n| H3 (C3 vs. C1) | Wilcoxon signed-rank, two-sided | paired bootstrap |\\n\\n**Multiple-comparison correction.** Holm–Bonferroni across {H1, H1c, H2, H3} at family-wise α = 0.05.\\n\\n**Effect sizes.** Cohen's d for paired proportions (mean Δ / sd(Δ)) on the per-task median scores; Cliff's δ as the non-parametric backup if Δ is heavy-tailed. 95% bootstrap CIs on Δ with 10,000 resamples (task-resampling preserves within-task pairing).\\n\\n**Disagreement-narration rule (H1 vs. H1c).** When H1 and H1c disagree (one significant positive, the other not), the headline paragraph narrates this using: (i) tier-by-tier Δ tables, (ii) the dominance plot (described in §A), and (iii) the numeric spend ratio = median tokens(C1) / median tokens(C0). No claim of \\\"improvement\\\" is made on H1c alone if H1 fails; each is reported with its own corrected p-value.\\n\\n**Stratified breakdown by tier.** A tier × cell interaction test (Friedman-style over tasks within tier, block by cell) is the primary heterogeneity report. The narrative explicitly states which tiers move Δ in which direction.\\n\\n**Ablations (pre-registered).**\\n\\n| Ablation | What it isolates | Required run |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| A1 few-shot count k ∈ {1, 3, 5} | exemplar dose–response on success and on token cost | adds 2×(k small + k large) cells within C2 |\\n| A2 prompt-length-matched sub-analysis | replace C1's think block with same-length filler before action | a C0-like arm re-using C1's exact slot size |\\n| A3 output-cap doubling (192 → 384 tok) | whether CoT was cap-limited | a parallel cell C1' running the same prompt with doubled cap |\\n| A4 drop C4 (or include if length-matchable) | tool-augmented scratchpad cost/value | only if §C gate passes |\\n| A5 calibration volume check (Brier) | whether identical \\\"done-confidence\\\" instruction produces non-degenerate confidences in all cells | one prompt-add to every cell |\\n\\n**Robustness replications.** A 10% subsample of tasks is re-run from scratch with fresh seeds per cell, and the headline Δs are recomputed. A successful robustness replication requires the original Δ direction to hold on the subsample.\\n\\n## H. Power and Sample-Size — Planning Sketch\\n\\n**Sample-size philosophy (functional).** Because no run has been executed, this is a planning sketch that is contingent on ^**assumption Z1 / pending confirmation**: per-tier task counts once the §B tier list is ratified. The design treats tiers as stratifying blocks; sample-size allocation is proportional to tier difficulty (T5 gets the most trials per task because expected variance is largest there), with a floor of 20 tasks per tier for the headline contrasts.\\n\\n**Closed-form power for paired Δ_success.** Treating each task's median outcome as a Bernoulli with success probability `p_i` per cell, and Δ as fixed across tasks, the effective paired test on n tasks has detection power roughly governed by:\\n- effect Δ = 7 pp *(assumption Z2: planning target in the 5–10 pp band; pending confirmation that this is realistic for the chosen model — see below)*\\n- baseline proportion p₀ ≈ 0.5 *(assumption Z3: planning target; pending confirmation by a pre-registered pilot)*\\n- SD(Δ) ≈ √(2p₀(1−p₀) − 2·ρ·√(p₀q₀·p₁q₁)) with ρ ≈ 0.6 *(assumption Z4: high within-task correlation under matched-pairs)*\\n- α = 0.05 after Holm over 4 tests → per-comparison α ≈ 0.0127\\n- power target 0.80\\n\\nUnder these assumptions, a normal-approximation calculation gives **n ≈ 60 tasks per tier for the H1 contrast**, ≈ 80 per tier if ρ drops to 0.4. The 10% robustness replication borrows no power; it is run on top of n.\\n\\n**Per-tier allocation (planning).**\\n\\n| Tier | Planned tasks (n) | Why |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| T1 list/lookup | 40 | low variance ceiling; smaller n is sufficient |\\n| T2 search/navigation | 60 | nominal |\\n| T3 forms | 60 | nominal |\\n| T4 configuration | 60 | nominal; expect lower p₀ so Δ detection harder |\\n| T5 composite / plan | 80 | higher variance, headroom for ceiling effects |\\n| **Total** | **≈ 300 tasks** | across cells × 3 trials ⇒ ≈ 3,600 trials |\\n\\nThese n's are placeholders. The locked pre-execution document replaces them with the actual flowing numbers once the task pool sizes are known.\\n\\n**On the choice of Δ = 7 pp as a planning target.** Neither prior-paper magnitudes nor task-id-specific numbers are cited here because they are not verifiable from the supplied workspace. The 7 pp band is a *planning convenience that's plausibly detectable* with n ≈ 60 tasks per tier, paired; if the actual model-level baseline variance is much lower (a high-performing model), a 3 pp Δ becomes detectable with the same n; if much higher, the design accepts lower power on smaller Δ and reports it in §I as a statistical-conclusion threat.\\n\\n## I. Threats to Validity (each tied to a mitigation already in the design)\\n\\n| Threat class | Specific threat | Mitigation built into the design |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Construct | CoT leak from the prompt scaffold into C0 (instruction artefact) | The C0 canary task (§F) flags any > 2 pp drift from C1-instruction-C0-prompt vs. plain C0; affected tasks are quarantined. |\\n| Construct | \\\"Improvement\\\" conflates success with cost | Δ_success and Δ_cost-adjusted score are both pre-registered co-primaries (§A); a single-tier breakdown shows where they disagree. |\\n| Internal | Task-content leakage from C2 exemplars into test tasks | Exemplar pool is built **before** the test set and is verified disjoint by id (§D); the canary audit runs in CI. |\\n| Internal | First-trial contamination from random seeding | Latin square per task (§D); \\\"first trial of session\\\" included as a covariate in §A. |\\n| Internal | Infrastructure flakiness mistaken for agent failure | Trial rerun policy (§D) flags infra errors distinctly from LLM-side failures. |\\n| Internal | Verbosity confounded with reasoning presence | Output token cap identical across cells; matched-verbosity filler in C0; A2 length-matched sub-analysis (§G). |\\n| External | One model, one prompt template | Documented as a §L limitation; out of scope to fix without a model-×-condition design that quadruples compute. |\\n| External | ServiceNow / BrowserGym version drift across runs | BrowserGym + WorkArena++ versions are pinned in `model.lock` (§C). |\\n| External | en-US locale only | Documented in §L. |\\n| Stat. conclusion | Multiple comparisons across 4 hypotheses | Holm–Bonferroni across {H1, H1c, H2, H3} (§G). |\\n| Stat. conclusion | Non-normality of paired Δ on small per-tier n | Wilcoxon as primary; Cliff's δ as non-parametric backup; bootstrap CIs. |\\n| Stat. conclusion | Calibration metric non-identifiability if \\\"done-confidence\\\" prompt is not actually attended to | A5 explicitly checks that all cells emit non-degenerate confidences before any Brier comparison. |\\n| Stat. conclusion | Pilot-derived p₀ assumption wrong by a wide margin | Power is recomputed from a small pilot (10 tasks) before main run; n is re-allocated if needed. |\\n\\n## J. Feasibility and Compute\\n\\n**Token-volume order-of-magnitude estimate [assumption Z5 / pending confirmation]**, per trial:\\n- prompt input per step ≈ 4,000 tokens *(assumption)*\\n- output per step: C0 ≈ 50 tok, C1/C2 ≈ 170 tok, C3 ≈ 170 tok × 5 samples = 850 tok *(assumption)*\\n- steps per trial: median ≈ 10, max 25\\n\\nPer-trial tokens ≈ 4,000 × 10 + output ≈ 40,000 in + 850–1,700 out for CoT arms; ≈ 40,000 in + 500 out for C0. **Per-trial spend is dominated by input; the C0/C1 token-ratio is ≈ 1.0–1.04×** at the per-trial level. C3 multiplies output ≈5×, but output is small relative to input.\\n\\n**Total budget sketch (using §H's n = 300 tasks, 3 trials, 4 cells).**\\n\\n| Cell | Trials | Approx. trials × tokens (out) | Approx. trials × tokens (in) |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| C0 | 900 | ≈ 0.5 M | ≈ 36 M |\\n| C1 | 900 | ≈ 1.5 M | ≈ 36 M |\\n| C2 | 900 | ≈ 1.5 M | ≈ 36 M |\\n| C3 | 900 | ≈ 7.5 M | ≈ 36 M (output ×5 dominates) |\\n\\n[assumption Z6 / pending confirmation] At an indicative frontier-model rate of ~$5/Mtok in / ~$15/Mtok out, total wall spend across all four cells is on the order of **low-to-mid single-digit USD-thousands**. This is a planning sketch whose numeric values are explicitly dependent on Z5/Z6.\\n\\n**ServiceNow / BrowserGym provisioning.** 24 CPU cores ⇒ run 24 BrowserGym jobs in parallel (one BrowserGym worker per SessionNow PDI or shared-tenant snapshot). One ServiceNow tenant per worker avoids cross-instance interference; a pre-execution fixture script snapshots the post-login state and is replayed before every trial (§D).\\n\\n**Time budget [assumption Z7].** Average wall-clock per trial ≈ 2–5 min *(assumption — pending pilot)*. With 24 parallel workers and 3,600 trials, the run completes in roughly **5–13 hours of wall time**. The bottleneck is BrowserGym rendering latency, which limit-parallelism gives directly.\\n\\n**Excluded from the budget:** human-labour for failure-mode coding (~300–900 failed trials expected; coded by 1 reviewer + audit sub-sample) is a small, separate cost.\\n\\n## K. Pre-Registration Checklist\\n\\n| Status | Item |\\n|---|---|\\n| **Fixed (locked before any execution)** | The four cells C0/C1/C2/C3 and their literal prompt slot diffs (§C); the link budget / output cap; tier list (§B); exemplar-pool disjointness rule and audit; primary test family + Holm over {H1, H1c, H2, H3}; co-primary endpoint set; canary task design; seed policy; LLM id pinned in `model.lock`. |\\n| **Fixed + subject to pilot-driven n adjustment** | Per-tier sample-size n (§H); the pilot recomputes power and is the only path by which n can change. |\\n| **Fixed + conditional** | Including C4 (tool-augmented scratchpad) only if §C length-match passes; otherwise C4 is dropped, not approximated. |\\n| **Exploratory, labelled as such** | A1 k-dosing sweep (k ∈ {1,3,5}); A3 output-cap doubling; A5 Brier volume check; failure-mode-rate stratification by tier beyond the planned interaction test; any post-hoc tier split of T5. |\\n| **Not in scope** | Cross-model replication; non-English locales; human-in-the-loop variants; cost-savings via model distillation; comparison to non-CoT plan-and-solve prompting families. |\\n\\n## L. Limitations\\n\\n1. **Single-model design.** External validity is bounded to the locked model id; the answer may not transfer, and the design acknowledges this without trying to fix it.\\n2. **Single prompt template.** The conclusions are conditional on the §C template; small prompt-engineering changes beyond the `<R>` slot could move the headline.\\n3. **English-only ServiceNow locale.** Other locales may have different ax-tree noise characteristics; not measured.\\n4. **Evaluator-LLM bias in failure coding.** If a judge LLM is the second coder (§E) its biases are reported alongside κ; the primary coder is human.\\n5. **No human-in-the-loop.** All actions are model-emitted; request-for-help behavior is not modelled.\\n6. **Task-solving vs. generalization.** The design measures performance on the benchmark's own tasks; it does **not** measure generalization to unseen ServiceNow tenants, custom instances, or post-upgrade environments.\\n7. **Source-verifiability.** No WorkArena++ task ID, no specific prior-paper success-rate number, and no model version string from outside the supplied workspace is invoked as evidence; every such detail is treated as a researcher-ratified parameter to be locked before execution.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Summary for the Reviewer\\n\\nThis design is a complete, executable, design-only specification for a matched-pairs experiment that asks whether CoT prompting improves LLM-agent task-solving on WorkArena++. With the C0/C1 split as the canonical headline and cost-adjusted success as the co-primary pre-registered alongside it, with Holm–Bonferroni over {H1, H1c, H2, H3}, with paired Wilcoxon tests and bootstrap CIs on Δ, with matched-verbosity fillers, a leak-canary, exemplar-pool disjointness, prompt-version pinning, Latin-square ordering, and a 10% robustness replication on fresh seeds — the experiment WOULD settle, with pre-registered decision rules per hypothesis in §A and a pre-registered dominance plot to narrate any success-vs-cost disagreement, whether C1 outperforms C0 on this fixed model and prompt template. Secondary cells C2 (k=3 few-shot) and C3 (N=5 self-consistency at T=0.7) are scored in the same Holm family and pre-registered ablations A1–A5 dissect the result along prompt-length, exemplar dose, scratchpad, and calibration axes. Every concrete number tied to an unverified assumption (task counts, prompt/input token sizes, exact $/Mtok rates, expected per-trial wall-clock, effective Δ detection threshold) is annotated *[assumption X / pending confirmation]* and is the locked-parameter list that, when finalized, fully specifies execution. No measurement is, or is presented as, observed.\",\"evidence\":\"Design-only output, grounded in (a) the workspace inspection (`ls -la /workspace` returned only `task.json`; `find / -maxdepth 3 -type f` produced no other project files; `cat /workspace/task.json` showed the question, the \\\"design-only\\\" context note, and a `track.md` value that is a one-line placeholder not a fetched document; `find / -name track.md` and `find / -path '*neurips*' -name '*.md'` returned no hits; `which python3` was unavailable and `pip3 list` showed no WorkArena/BrowserGym packages, matching `task.json`'s `network: disabled`, `gpu_enabled: false`, `software_environment: \\\"system Python; project dependencies not preinstalled\\\"` settings), and (b) the researcher's explicit resolutions in this turn to the two top uncertainties: (1) PRIMARY ENDPOINT = paired task-level success-rate Δ as the headline, with cost-adjusted success per 1k tokens as a co-primary in the same Holm family (no quality gate requiring both); (2) SCOPE = C1 vs. C0 as the canonical headline, C2 (k=3) and C3 (N=5, T=0.7) as secondaries in the same factorial, C4 optional and only if it length-matches C1 — otherwise dropped. The researcher also locked single fixed base model across all cells (model id to be chosen/pinned by the design and ratified by the researcher; documented as default proposal pending confirmation), the <think>…</think> channel being logged, and the requirement that the design be self-contained, executable by a third party, and free of unverifiable numeric citations or task IDs. All such numbers in the design are flagged inline as [assumption X / pending confirmation] (Z1–Z7) per the researcher's hard constraint (iii). No execution was attempted; the run-time environment is offline (no network, no GPU, no WorkArena/BrowserGym installation), which is consistent with the design-only deliverable and does not constitute evidence that \\\"the experiment ran.\\\"\",\"assumptions\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"English-only ServiceNow locale; no human-in-the-loop; no cross-tenant generalization claimed.</item>\",\"$text\":\"No specific WorkArena++ task ID, no specific prior-paper numeric baseline, and no specific model version string from outside the supplied workspace is used as evidence anywhere in the design.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"WorkArena++ is assumed to be the BrowserGym-served ServiceNow benchmark; this is the researcher's framing and not independently verified from the workspace.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"§J per-trial wall-clock 2–5 min (Z7) is a planning assumption pending pilot.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"§J $5/Mtok in / $15/Mtok out (Z6) is a placeholder indicative rate; exact rate depends on the locked model id.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"§J prompt input ≈ 4,000 tok/step, output per cell as stated, median ≈ 10 steps/trial (Z5) are planning assumptions pending pilot confirmation.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"§H Per-tier n allocation (40/60/60/60/80, ≈300 total) is a planning sketch whose effective values depend on Z1.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"§H Per-tier task counts (Z1) and the planning Δ = 7 pp (Z2) and p₀ ≈ 0.5 (Z3) and ρ ≈ 0.6 (Z4) are all pending confirmation by a pre-execution pilot.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"§G Holm family size of 4 hypotheses is pre-registered; if C4 is dropped the family shrinks accordingly.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"§F Canary-task drift threshold of 2 pp is an operational placeholder pending sensitivity check during pilot.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"§C BrowserGym release is the most recent stable release shipping with the adopted WorkArena++; exact version pending confirmation.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"§C Base model default is a current frontier chat model in the GPT-4o / Claude-3.5-Sonnet class; specific model id, version, and API access are pending confirmation and will be pinned in model.lock before execution.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"§B Tier list (T1 list/lookup, T2 search/navigation, T3 forms, T4 configuration, T5 composite/plan) is a candidate list; the exact WorkArena++ tier taxonomy is ratified-by-researcher and pending confirmation.</item>\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"Whether Brier calibration yields non-degenerate confidences in all cells (A5 dependency); if not, calibration is dropped from secondary endpoints rather than reported on a biased sample.</item>\",\"$text\":\"Whether WorkArena++ ships a train/test split or whether the §D exemplar-pool disjointness rule needs to rely on a frozen carve-out.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Whether the planned 5–10 pp Δ detection band is realistic for the chosen model; pilot pre-execution will revise §H if not.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Whether the §F canary-task drift threshold (2 pp) is the right operating point; sensitised during pilot.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Whether §C's length-match gate for C4 will pass on the actual prompts; otherwise C4 is dropped per the researcher's instruction.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"How many tasks are in each ratified tier (§B)? Without Z1 the §H n-allocations are placeholders.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Will the researcher ratify the §C default model id or substitute a different one? This pins model.lock and the §J $/Mtok budget.</item>\"}]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-70aae053-476a-4743-aa09-fe5c18dfe607", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 19}
{"id": "aab11195-6a13-4418-ae30-b27f98a21e5a", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T15:41:33.371707", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "df6c7f65-36b7-4d25-b285-acfe0050498a", "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-408116b6-0c9b-4a93-bf9e-000d74e7a75f", "error": "Error validating tool 'end_researcher_turn': 2 validation errors for ReviewHandoffAction\nassumptions.0\n  Input should be a valid string [type=string_type, input_value={'item': {'item': {'item'...g confirmation.</item>'}, input_type=dict]\n    For further information visit https://errors.pydantic.dev/2.13/v/string_type\nuncertainties.0\n  Input should be a valid string [type=string_type, input_value={'item': {'item': {'item'... $/Mtok budget.</item>'}, input_type=dict]\n    For further information visit https://errors.pydantic.dev/2.13/v/string_type. Parameters provided: ['stage', 'answer', 'evidence', 'assumptions', 'uncertainties']", "classification": {"kind": "agent_action", "retryable": true, "user_action": "retry", "error_id": null}, "kind": "AgentErrorEvent", "source_event_index": 20}
{"id": "28743262-8720-435b-ad06-d473d24041e2", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T15:43:08.418652", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "aab11195-6a13-4418-ae30-b27f98a21e5a", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-e8baafa4-b155-4fb8-b20e-f0769751b5e5", "tool_call": {"id": "call-e8baafa4-b155-4fb8-b20e-f0769751b5e5", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Full design result with 12 sections + reviewer summary\",\"stage\":\"result\",\"answer\":\"# Experiment Design - Does Chain-of-Thought (CoT) Prompting Improve LLM-Agent Task-Solving in WorkArena++?\\n\\nThis is a design-only deliverable. Nothing has been executed and no measurements are reported. Every concrete number that depends on an unverified assumption is flagged inline as [assumption X / pending confirmation].\\n\\n## A. Research Question and Operational Hypotheses\\n\\nResearch question (operational). Within a fixed agent stack and a fixed base LLM, does inserting explicit chain-of-thought (CoT) reasoning immediately before action emission improve task-solving on WorkArena++?\\n\\n| # | Hypothesis | Test direction | One-line pre-registered decision rule |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| H1 (primary, headline) | CoT (C1) greater than No-CoT (C0) on paired task-level success rate. | one-sided, delta greater than 0 | Declare C1 improves over C0 iff (i) Wilcoxon signed-rank p less than or equal to alpha after Holm-Bonferroni, (ii) paired delta greater than 0, and (iii) its 95% bootstrap CI excludes 0 with at least 80% of resampled mass above 0. |\\n| H1c (co-primary) | C1 greater than C0 on cost-adjusted success (success per 1k total tokens). | one-sided, delta greater than 0 | Same template as H1, applied to the second metric in the same Holm family. Two co-primaries are reported; a disagreement is narrated by tier-stratified delta and a dominance plot, not by gating them. |\\n| H2 (secondary) | C2 (Few-shot CoT, k=3) differs from C1. | two-sided | Report sign, magnitude, and Holm-adjusted p; the narrative question is whether few-shot exemplars add, subtract, or match zero-shot CoT. |\\n| H3 (secondary) | C3 (Self-consistency, N=5, T=0.7) differs from C1. | two-sided | Report sign, magnitude, and Holm-adjusted p; the narrative question is whether the five-times token spend yields a measurable success gain over C1. |\\n\\nHolm family for primary inference equals {H1, H1c, H2, H3}. A pre-registered dominance plot (delta success on x-axis, delta cost-adjusted score on y-axis, one point per tier) is the canonical visual for telling the success-vs-cost story.\\n\\n## B. Platform - WorkArena++ Tier Taxonomy\\n\\nWorkArena++ is assumed to be the BrowserGym-served ServiceNow benchmark. The exact tier list is ratified-by-researcher and pending confirmation; no specific task IDs from the suite are used in this design.\\n\\nAdopted candidate tier list (the design treats these as parameters to be locked pre-execution):\\n\\n| Tier code | Candidate name | Example capability | Sample-size stratum |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| T1 | list / lookup | read-only retrieval on ServiceNow records | small-to-mid |\\n| T2 | search / navigation | multi-page traversal, filter/sort | mid |\\n| T3 | forms | create or edit record via form fields | mid |\\n| T4 | configuration | change system settings or workflows | small |\\n| T5 | composite or plan | multi-step, cross-page objectives | small (hardest) |\\n\\nIf the ratified list differs, the only knob that must change is per-tier sample allocation in Section H; everything downstream is tier-symbolic. Counters external to the design (such as composite plan tasks) are revisited once the ratified tier set is in hand.\\n\\n## C. Agent Stack\\n\\nBase LLM. Single fixed chat model across all cells. Default proposal, pending confirmation of API access and pinning: a current frontier chat model in the GPT-4o or Claude-3.5-Sonnet class with at least 128k context, function- or tool-calling disabled (WorkArena++ uses a discrete action enum, not tool calls). The exact model id is pinned in a single model.lock file before execution and never changed mid-run. All four cells C0, C1, C2, C3 use that same id.\\n\\nBrowserGym / ServiceNow. BrowserGym (release: assumption - use the most recent stable BrowserGym that ships with the adopted WorkArena++; pending confirmation) over headless Chromium controlling a ServiceNow Personal Developer Instance or shared testing tenant. Assumption: one instance per worker; instance snapshot id is fixed. UI locale: en-US (see Section L).\\n\\nObservation to prompt interface.\\n- Page observation equals pruned HTML accessibility tree (DOM-pruned to interactive and labelled elements), capped at about 6k tokens.\\n- Action format equals BrowserGym's discrete enum (for example: click with element id, type with element id and text, select, navigate, done).\\n- Prompt payload per step equals the tuple: system, goal, observation tree, last action, last error, chat history of (thought, action, observation) tuples. History is windowed to the last K turns (K = 10 by default) to control prompt size.\\n\\nCoT insertion slot. A single think-block immediately before the action, channel-separated and logged verbatim for failure-mode coding (Section E).\\n\\nLength-control mechanism. All four cells are constrained to a per-step output budget of at most 192 tokens total (think plus action combined), enforced by a stop sequence and a downstream parser. This makes CoT-length an independent variable measured rather than a confound allowed to drift. The Section G ablation explicitly tests what happens when this cap is doubled.\\n\\nDecoding settings.\\n\\n| Cell | Temperature | Top-p | Number of samples | Aggregation | Seed |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| C0 No-CoT | 0 | 1.0 | 1 | n/a | fixed |\\n| C1 Zero-shot CoT | 0 | 1.0 | 1 | n/a | fixed |\\n| C2 Few-shot CoT | 0 | 1.0 | 1 | n/a | fixed |\\n| C3 Self-consistency | 0.7 | 1.0 | 5 | majority action (think discarded) | fixed, plus per-sample index |\\n\\nPrompt template skeleton (literal slots). Slots R and F are the only differences across cells; everything else is identical.\\n\\nSYSTEM:\\nYou are an agent controlling a ServiceNow instance. You observe an accessibility\\ntree, you think if asked, and you emit exactly one BrowserGym action. Never exceed\\n192 output tokens.\\n\\nGOAL:\\n{goal}\\n\\nOBSERVATION:\\n{ax_tree}\\n\\nLAST_ACTION: {last_action}\\nLAST_ERROR:  {last_error}\\n\\nR is the reasoning-instruction block (differs per cell; see below).\\nF is the exemplar block (only present in C2).\\n\\nNOW EMIT ONE ACTION.\\n\\nR per cell:\\n\\n| Cell | R block (literal) |\\n|---|---|\\n| C0 | Plan in one short sentence, then act. This is a no-reasoning filler to match verbosity. |\\n| C1 | Think step by step about the goal and the current page before acting. |\\n| C2 | inherits C1 R, exact same text |\\n| C3 | inherits C1 R, exact same text; only temperature and N change |\\n\\nF per cell:\\n\\n| Cell | F block |\\n|---|---|\\n| C0 | n/a |\\n| C1 | n/a |\\n| C2 | k=3 worked think-action-observation exemplars drawn from the same tier, disjoint from the test set |\\n| C3 | n/a (no exemplars; reasoning sampled 5 times) |\\n\\nC4 (tool-augmented scratchpad) is included only if its R can be length-matched to C1 within plus or minus 10 percent of output-token expectation on a 50-step dry run; otherwise it is dropped from the run. The design is built so C4 can be added or removed without touching anything else.\\n\\n## D. Protocol\\n\\n| Knob | Value | Rationale |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Trials per task times cell | 3 | absorbs stochasticity for paired tests |\\n| Median of 3 trials used as task score | yes | reduces variance without inflating matched-pairs design |\\n| Max steps per trial | 25 (T5: 40) | tier-specific cap |\\n| Wall-clock timeout per trial | 10 min | aborts hung agents |\\n| Per-step HTTP timeout | 30 s | protects against infinite loads |\\n| Retry policy inside a trial | none | a step retry would break paired integrity |\\n| Trial rerun on infra error | at most 1, flagged | distinguishes LLM-side from infra-side failures |\\n| Environment reset | hard rollback to a known post-login snapshot before every trial |\\n| Reset sentinel | assert that a ready-element is present after reset; otherwise abort trial |\\n| Randomization | Latin-square ordering of cells across tasks | controls first-trial contamination |\\n| Exemplar pool (C2) | 5 held-out exemplar tasks per tier, disjoint from test tasks by construction; pool is fixed and published |\\n| Exemplar sampling per trial | each trial draws k=3 without replacement from the tier pool; draw is randomized per trial but pool is fixed |\\n| Seed hygiene | Python, NumPy, the LLM SDK, and BrowserGym are all seeded; seeds are logged per trial |\\n\\nExemplar-pool disjointness rule (C2). The exemplar pool is constructed once, before any test task is sampled, by selecting k_pool=5 demonstration tasks per tier from the WorkArena++ training portion or, if the suite does not ship a train/test split, from a frozen-enough carve-out. The test task set is then drawn strictly disjoint from this pool by id. A single-bit audit (exemplar_in_test_set leads to run abort) is in CI before any result is reported. This guarantees C2 cannot leak task content into the test set.\\n\\n## E. Metrics\\n\\n| Metric | Tier | Definition | Unit |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| success | primary (H1) | Indicator that the trial reached the task's terminal done with the success predicate true on the post-condition check. Per trial; median of 3 per task times cell. | 0/1 |\\n| delta_success | primary | Paired (task-level) C1 minus C0 on the median-success-per-task summary. | proportion points |\\n| cost-adjusted score | co-primary (H1c) | success per 1,000 total tokens (input plus output, summed over the whole trial). | 1 / 1k tok |\\n| delta_cost-adjusted | co-primary | Paired C1 minus C0 on cost-adjusted score. | 1 / 1k tok |\\n| steps | secondary | Median steps-to-success over the 3 trials; for failed trials equals max_steps plus 1 (censored). | int |\\n| tokens_in | secondary | Sum of prompt tokens across all turns in a trial. | tokens |\\n| tokens_out | secondary | Sum of completion tokens across all turns in a trial. | tokens |\\n| dollar per success | secondary | (tokens_in times cost_in plus tokens_out times cost_out) divided by success. | USD |\\n| latency | secondary | Wall-clock seconds per trial and per step. | s |\\n| hallucination rate | secondary | Per-trial indicator that any step's think references a UI element not in the ax-tree. | 0/1 |\\n| loop rate | secondary | Per-trial indicator of at least 2 consecutive identical state-action pairs. | 0/1 |\\n| failure-mode code | secondary | One of 6 codes, labelled on every failed trial (taxonomy below). | categorical |\\n| Brier on done-confidence | secondary | Optional. The agent emits a 0 to 1 done-confidence before each done action; Brier equals mean of (confidence minus actual_success) squared. The instruction to emit this confidence is identical across cells. | score |\\n\\nFailure-mode taxonomy (6 codes). A failed trial is assigned exactly one code by manual review, with the think log as primary evidence:\\n\\n1. wrong_goal - agent misinterpreted the instruction.\\n2. hallucinated_element - agent referenced a UI element not in the ax-tree.\\n3. action_format_error - agent emitted a malformed action.\\n4. loop - agent repeated the same state-action.\\n5. premature_stop - agent called done before goal was actually achieved.\\n6. post_condition_check_failed - agent thought it was done but the predicate failed.\\n\\nInter-rater reliability is reported for the subset of failures double-coded by a second human; agreement target is Cohen kappa at least 0.7. If a judge LLM is used as the second coder (allowed only at this auditing step, not as the primary coder), the prompt is fixed and its outputs are reported alongside kappa to keep evaluator bias visible.\\n\\n## F. Controls and Confounders\\n\\n| Confounder | Control rule in the design |\\n|---|---|\\n| Prompt length as free parameter | C0 is given a matched-verbosity filler (\\\"Plan in one short sentence, then act\\\"). Output cap (192 tok) is identical across all cells. A length-matched sub-analysis in Section G explicitly swaps CoT for filler to test whether any effect survives. |\\n| CoT leakage into C0 (instruction artefact) | Canary task: 10 atomically-short look-up tasks are run with C0's prompt but with C1's literal R block pasted in verbatim. If the canary shows greater than 2 pp drift vs. plain C0, the architecture leaks and the design quarantines those 10 tasks out of H1 inference. |\\n| Temperature drift between deterministic cells | C0, C1, C2 all run at T=0; only C3 switches to T=0.7. The cost of C3 is recorded separately and not silently averaged into the headline. |\\n| Tool or capability parity | All cells use the same BrowserGym action enum; function-calling, retrieval, and web search are disabled across the board. |\\n| Caching | LLM call-side caching is disabled; static prompt-template caching on the client side is enabled. Each trial's exact tokens are re-counted in post-processing. |\\n| Prompt-version pinning | Every prompt template is stored under a versioned path. The execution harness refuses to start if the date stamp on the loaded prompt differs from the one in model.lock. No mid-run edits are permitted. |\\n| Exemplar-pool identity | Frozen before any test task is sampled (see Section D). |\\n| Order effects | Latin square across cells within each task. The first-trial contamination effect is tested as a covariate in Section G. |\\n| Random seed reproducibility | All RNGs seeded and logged per trial; failure to reproduce a trial from logs is itself a logged event. |\\n\\n## G. Analysis Plan\\n\\nStatistical unit. The task. One observation per task times cell is the median of 3 trials.\\n\\nPairing. Every task sees every cell (within-subject). Pairing is therefore at the task level.\\n\\nPrimary test family.\\n\\n| Hypothesis | Primary test | Robustness tiebreaker |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| H1 (success delta) | Wilcoxon signed-rank, one-sided C1 greater than C0 | McNemar on per-trial success counts (paired binomial) |\\n| H1c (cost-adjusted delta) | Wilcoxon signed-rank, one-sided C1 greater than C0 | paired bootstrap on the score |\\n| H2 (C2 vs C1) | Wilcoxon signed-rank, two-sided | paired bootstrap |\\n| H3 (C3 vs C1) | Wilcoxon signed-rank, two-sided | paired bootstrap |\\n\\nMultiple-comparison correction. Holm-Bonferroni across {H1, H1c, H2, H3} at family-wise alpha equals 0.05.\\n\\nEffect sizes. Cohen's d for paired proportions (mean delta divided by sd of delta) on the per-task median scores; Cliff's delta as the non-parametric backup if delta is heavy-tailed. 95% bootstrap CIs on delta with 10,000 resamples (task-resampling preserves within-task pairing).\\n\\nDisagreement-narration rule (H1 vs H1c). When H1 and H1c disagree (one significant positive, the other not), the headline paragraph narrates this using: (i) tier-by-tier delta tables, (ii) the dominance plot (described in Section A), and (iii) the numeric spend ratio equals median tokens(C1) divided by median tokens(C0). No claim of improvement is made on H1c alone if H1 fails; each is reported with its own corrected p-value.\\n\\nStratified breakdown by tier. A tier times cell interaction test (Friedman-style over tasks within tier, block by cell) is the primary heterogeneity report. The narrative explicitly states which tiers move delta in which direction.\\n\\nAblations (pre-registered).\\n\\n| Ablation | What it isolates | Required run |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| A1 few-shot count k in {1, 3, 5} | exemplar dose-response on success and on token cost | adds 2 extra cells within C2 |\\n| A2 prompt-length-matched sub-analysis | replace C1's think block with same-length filler before action | a C0-like arm re-using C1's exact slot size |\\n| A3 output-cap doubling (192 to 384 tok) | whether CoT was cap-limited | a parallel cell C1-prime running the same prompt with doubled cap |\\n| A4 drop C4 (or include if length-matchable) | tool-augmented scratchpad cost/value | only if Section C gate passes |\\n| A5 calibration volume check (Brier) | whether identical done-confidence instruction produces non-degenerate confidences in all cells | one prompt-add to every cell |\\n\\nRobustness replications. A 10 percent subsample of tasks is re-run from scratch with fresh seeds per cell, and the headline deltas are recomputed. A successful robustness replication requires the original delta direction to hold on the subsample.\\n\\n## H. Power and Sample-Size - Planning Sketch\\n\\nSample-size philosophy (functional). Because no run has been executed, this is a planning sketch that is contingent on assumption Z1 (pending confirmation): per-tier task counts once the Section B tier list is ratified. The design treats tiers as stratifying blocks; sample-size allocation is proportional to tier difficulty (T5 gets the most trials per task because expected variance is largest there), with a floor of 20 tasks per tier for the headline contrasts.\\n\\nClosed-form power for paired delta success. Treating each task's median outcome as a Bernoulli with success probability p_i per cell, the effective paired test on n tasks has detection power roughly governed by:\\n- effect delta equals 7 pp (assumption Z2: planning target in the 5 to 10 pp band; pending confirmation that this is realistic for the chosen model)\\n- baseline proportion p_0 about 0.5 (assumption Z3: planning target; pending confirmation by a pre-registered pilot)\\n- sd(delta) about sqrt of (2 p_0 times (1 minus p_0) minus 2 rho times sqrt of (p_0 q_0 times p_1 q_1))) with rho about 0.6 (assumption Z4: high within-task correlation under matched-pairs)\\n- alpha equals 0.05 after Holm over 4 tests leads to per-comparison alpha about 0.0127\\n- power target 0.80\\n\\nUnder these assumptions, a normal-approximation calculation gives n about 60 tasks per tier for the H1 contrast, and about 80 per tier if rho drops to 0.4. The 10 percent robustness replication borrows no power; it is run on top of n.\\n\\nPer-tier allocation (planning).\\n\\n| Tier | Planned tasks (n) | Why |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| T1 list/lookup | 40 | low variance ceiling; smaller n is sufficient |\\n| T2 search/navigation | 60 | nominal |\\n| T3 forms | 60 | nominal |\\n| T4 configuration | 60 | nominal; expect lower p_0 so delta detection harder |\\n| T5 composite or plan | 80 | higher variance, headroom for ceiling effects |\\n| Total | about 300 tasks | across cells times 3 trials leads to about 3600 trials |\\n\\nThese n's are placeholders. The locked pre-execution document replaces them with the actual flowing numbers once the task pool sizes are known.\\n\\nOn the choice of delta equals 7 pp as a planning target. Neither prior-paper magnitudes nor task-id-specific numbers are cited here because they are not verifiable from the supplied workspace. The 7 pp band is a planning convenience that is plausibly detectable with n about 60 tasks per tier, paired; if the actual model-level baseline variance is much lower (a high-performing model), a 3 pp delta becomes detectable with the same n; if much higher, the design accepts lower power on smaller delta and reports it in Section I as a statistical-conclusion threat.\\n\\n## I. Threats to Validity (each tied to a mitigation already in the design)\\n\\n| Threat class | Specific threat | Mitigation built into the design |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Construct | CoT leak from the prompt scaffold into C0 (instruction artefact) | The C0 canary task (Section F) flags any drift greater than 2 pp; affected tasks are quarantined. |\\n| Construct | Improvement conflates success with cost | delta_success and delta_cost-adjusted score are both pre-registered co-primaries (Section A); a single-tier breakdown shows where they disagree. |\\n| Internal | Task-content leakage from C2 exemplars into test tasks | Exemplar pool is built before the test set and is verified disjoint by id (Section D); the canary audit runs in CI. |\\n| Internal | First-trial contamination from random seeding | Latin square per task (Section D); first trial of session is a covariate in Section G. |\\n| Internal | Infrastructure flakiness mistaken for agent failure | Trial rerun policy (Section D) flags infra errors distinctly from LLM-side failures. |\\n| Internal | Verbosity confounded with reasoning presence | Output token cap identical across cells; matched-verbosity filler in C0; A2 length-matched sub-analysis (Section G). |\\n| External | One model, one prompt template | Documented as a Section L limitation; out of scope to fix without a model-times-condition design that quadruples compute. |\\n| External | ServiceNow or BrowserGym version drift across runs | BrowserGym and WorkArena++ versions are pinned in model.lock (Section C). |\\n| External | en-US locale only | Documented in Section L. |\\n| Stat. conclusion | Multiple comparisons across 4 hypotheses | Holm-Bonferroni across {H1, H1c, H2, H3} (Section G). |\\n| Stat. conclusion | Non-normality of paired delta on small per-tier n | Wilcoxon as primary; Cliff's delta as non-parametric backup; bootstrap CIs. |\\n| Stat. conclusion | Calibration metric non-identifiability if done-confidence prompt is not actually attended to | A5 explicitly checks that all cells emit non-degenerate confidences before any Brier comparison. |\\n| Stat. conclusion | Pilot-derived p_0 assumption wrong by a wide margin | Power is recomputed from a small pilot (10 tasks) before main run; n is re-allocated if needed. |\\n\\n## J. Feasibility and Compute\\n\\nToken-volume order-of-magnitude estimate (assumption Z5, pending confirmation), per trial:\\n- prompt input per step about 4000 tokens (assumption)\\n- output per step: C0 about 50 tok, C1 and C2 about 170 tok, C3 about 170 tok times 5 samples equals 850 tok (assumption)\\n- steps per trial: median about 10, max 25\\n\\nPer-trial tokens about 4000 times 10 plus output, which is about 40000 in plus 850 to 1700 out for CoT arms; about 40000 in plus 500 out for C0. Per-trial spend is dominated by input; the C0 and C1 token-ratio is about 1.0 to 1.04x at the per-trial level. C3 multiplies output about five times, but output is small relative to input.\\n\\nTotal budget sketch (using Section H n = 300 tasks, 3 trials, 4 cells).\\n\\n| Cell | Trials | Approx tokens (out) total | Approx tokens (in) total |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| C0 | 900 | about 0.5 M | about 36 M |\\n| C1 | 900 | about 1.5 M | about 36 M |\\n| C2 | 900 | about 1.5 M | about 36 M |\\n| C3 | 900 | about 7.5 M | about 36 M (output times 5 dominates) |\\n\\nAssumption Z6, pending confirmation: at an indicative frontier-model rate of about 5 USD per Mtok in and about 15 USD per Mtok out, total wall spend across all four cells is on the order of low-to-mid single-digit USD-thousands. This is a planning sketch whose numeric values are explicitly dependent on Z5 and Z6.\\n\\nServiceNow / BrowserGym provisioning. 24 CPU cores means run 24 BrowserGym jobs in parallel (one BrowserGym worker per ServiceNow PDI or shared-tenant snapshot). One ServiceNow tenant per worker avoids cross-instance interference; a pre-execution fixture script snapshots the post-login state and is replayed before every trial (Section D).\\n\\nTime budget (assumption Z7). Average wall-clock per trial about 2 to 5 min (assumption, pending pilot). With 24 parallel workers and 3600 trials, the run completes in roughly 5 to 13 hours of wall time. The bottleneck is BrowserGym rendering latency, which limit-parallelism gives directly.\\n\\nExcluded from the budget: human-labour for failure-mode coding (about 300 to 900 failed trials expected; coded by 1 reviewer plus audit sub-sample) is a small, separate cost.\\n\\n## K. Pre-Registration Checklist\\n\\n| Status | Item |\\n|---|---|\\n| Fixed (locked before any execution) | The four cells C0, C1, C2, C3 and their literal prompt slot diffs (Section C); the link budget and output cap; tier list (Section B); exemplar-pool disjointness rule and audit; primary test family plus Holm over {H1, H1c, H2, H3}; co-primary endpoint set; canary task design; seed policy; LLM id pinned in model.lock. |\\n| Fixed plus subject to pilot-driven n adjustment | Per-tier sample-size n (Section H); the pilot recomputes power and is the only path by which n can change. |\\n| Fixed plus conditional | Including C4 (tool-augmented scratchpad) only if Section C length-match passes; otherwise C4 is dropped, not approximated. |\\n| Exploratory, labelled as such | A1 k-dosing sweep (k in {1, 3, 5}); A3 output-cap doubling; A5 Brier volume check; failure-mode-rate stratification by tier beyond the planned interaction test; any post-hoc tier split of T5. |\\n| Not in scope | Cross-model replication; non-English locales; human-in-the-loop variants; cost-savings via model distillation; comparison to non-CoT plan-and-solve prompting families. |\\n\\n## L. Limitations\\n\\n1. Single-model design. External validity is bounded to the locked model id; the answer may not transfer, and the design acknowledges this without trying to fix it.\\n2. Single prompt template. The conclusions are conditional on the Section C template; small prompt-engineering changes beyond the R slot could move the headline.\\n3. English-only ServiceNow locale. Other locales may have different ax-tree noise characteristics; not measured.\\n4. Evaluator-LLM bias in failure coding. If a judge LLM is the second coder (Section E) its biases are reported alongside kappa; the primary coder is human.\\n5. No human-in-the-loop. All actions are model-emitted; request-for-help behavior is not modelled.\\n6. Task-solving versus generalization. The design measures performance on the benchmark's own tasks; it does not measure generalization to unseen ServiceNow tenants, custom instances, or post-upgrade environments.\\n7. Source-verifiability. No WorkArena++ task ID, no specific prior-paper success-rate number, and no model version string from outside the supplied workspace is invoked as evidence; every such detail is treated as a researcher-ratified parameter to be locked before execution.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Summary for the Reviewer\\n\\nThis design is a complete, executable, design-only specification for a matched-pairs experiment that asks whether CoT prompting improves LLM-agent task-solving on WorkArena++. With the C0/C1 split as the canonical headline and cost-adjusted success as the co-primary pre-registered alongside it, with Holm-Bonferroni over {H1, H1c, H2, H3}, with paired Wilcoxon tests and bootstrap CIs on delta, with matched-verbosity fillers, a leak-canary, exemplar-pool disjointness, prompt-version pinning, Latin-square ordering, and a 10 percent robustness replication on fresh seeds, the experiment WOULD settle, with pre-registered decision rules per hypothesis in Section A and a pre-registered dominance plot to narrate any success-vs-cost disagreement, whether C1 outperforms C0 on this fixed model and prompt template. Secondary cells C2 (k=3 few-shot) and C3 (N=5 self-consistency at T=0.7) are scored in the same Holm family and pre-registered ablations A1 to A5 dissect the result along prompt-length, exemplar dose, scratchpad, and calibration axes. Every concrete number tied to an unverified assumption (task counts, prompt and input token sizes, exact per-Mtok rates, expected per-trial wall-clock, effective delta detection threshold) is annotated as [assumption X / pending confirmation] and is the locked-parameter list that, when finalized, fully specifies execution. No measurement is, or is presented as, observed.\",\"evidence\":\"Design-only output, grounded in (a) the workspace inspection: ls -la /workspace returned only task.json; find with maxdepth 3 produced no other project files; cat /workspace/task.json showed the question, the design-only context note, and a track.md value that is a one-line placeholder not a fetched document; find / -name track.md and find / -path 'neurips*' -name '*.md' returned no hits; which python3 was unavailable and pip3 list showed no WorkArena or BrowserGym packages, matching task.json's network: disabled, gpu_enabled: false, software_environment: 'system Python; project dependencies not preinstalled' settings; and (b) the researcher's explicit resolutions in this turn to the two top uncertainties: (1) PRIMARY ENDPOINT equals paired task-level success-rate delta as the headline, with cost-adjusted success per 1k tokens as a co-primary in the same Holm family and no quality gate requiring both; (2) SCOPE equals C1 vs C0 as the canonical headline, C2 (k=3) and C3 (N=5, T=0.7) as secondaries in the same factorial, C4 optional and only if it length-matches C1 - otherwise dropped. The researcher also locked a single fixed base model across all cells (model id to be chosen or pinned by the design and ratified by the researcher; documented as default proposal pending confirmation), the think-channel being logged, and the requirement that the design be self-contained, executable by a third party, and free of unverifiable numeric citations or task IDs. All such numbers in the design are flagged inline as [assumption X / pending confirmation] (Z1-Z7) per the researcher's hard constraint (iii). The runtime environment is offline (no network, no GPU, no WorkArena/BrowserGym installation), which is consistent with the design-only deliverable and does not constitute evidence that the experiment ran.\",\"assumptions\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"Section B tier list (T1 list/lookup, T2 search/navigation, T3 forms, T4 configuration, T5 composite or plan) is a candidate list; the exact WorkArena++ tier taxonomy is ratified-by-researcher and pending confirmation.</item>\",\"$text\":\"Section C base model default is a current frontier chat model in the GPT-4o or Claude-3.5-Sonnet class; specific model id, version, and API access are pending confirmation and will be pinned in model.lock before execution.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Section C BrowserGym release is the most recent stable release shipping with the adopted WorkArena++; exact version pending confirmation.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Section F canary-task drift threshold of 2 pp is an operational placeholder pending sensitivity check during pilot.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Section G Holm family size of 4 hypotheses is pre-registered; if C4 is dropped the family shrinks accordingly.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Section H per-tier task counts (Z1), planning delta of 7 pp (Z2), p_0 about 0.5 (Z3), and rho about 0.6 (Z4) are all pending confirmation by a pre-execution pilot.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Section H per-tier n allocation 40/60/60/60/80, about 300 total, is a planning sketch whose effective values depend on Z1.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Section J prompt input about 4000 tok per step, output per cell as stated, median about 10 steps per trial (Z5) are planning assumptions pending pilot confirmation.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Section J indicative rate of about 5 USD per Mtok in and about 15 USD per Mtok out (Z6) is a placeholder; exact rate depends on the locked model id.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Section J per-trial wall-clock 2 to 5 min (Z7) is a planning assumption pending pilot.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"WorkArena++ is assumed to be the BrowserGym-served ServiceNow benchmark; this is the researcher's framing and not independently verified from the workspace.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"No specific WorkArena++ task ID, no specific prior-paper numeric baseline, and no specific model version string from outside the supplied workspace is used as evidence anywhere in the design.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"English-only ServiceNow locale; no human-in-the-loop; no cross-tenant generalization claimed.</item>\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"Will the researcher ratify the Section C default model id or substitute a different one? This pins model.lock and the Section J per-Mtok budget.</item>\",\"$text\":\"How many tasks are in each ratified tier (Section B)? Without Z1 the Section H n-allocations are placeholders.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Whether Section C length-match gate for C4 will pass on the actual prompts; otherwise C4 is dropped per researcher's instruction.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Whether the Section F canary-task drift threshold of 2 pp is the right operating point; sensitised during pilot.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Whether the planned 5 to 10 pp delta detection band is realistic for the chosen model; pilot pre-execution will revise Section H if not.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Whether WorkArena++ ships a train/test split or whether the Section D exemplar-pool disjointness rule needs to rely on a frozen carve-out.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Whether Brier calibration yields non-degenerate confidences in all cells (A5 dependency); if not, calibration is dropped from secondary endpoints rather than reported on a biased sample.</item>\"}]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-b79cce29-31ce-4873-a415-7bbf1f9edb4e", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 21}
{"id": "d77bf540-bfb9-4de4-9010-f845f980e5da", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T15:43:08.419502", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "28743262-8720-435b-ad06-d473d24041e2", "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-e8baafa4-b155-4fb8-b20e-f0769751b5e5", "error": "Error validating tool 'end_researcher_turn': 2 validation errors for ReviewHandoffAction\nassumptions.0\n  Input should be a valid string [type=string_type, input_value={'item': {'item': {'item'...zation claimed.</item>'}, input_type=dict]\n    For further information visit https://errors.pydantic.dev/2.13/v/string_type\nuncertainties.0\n  Input should be a valid string [type=string_type, input_value={'item': {'item': {'item'... biased sample.</item>'}, input_type=dict]\n    For further information visit https://errors.pydantic.dev/2.13/v/string_type. Parameters provided: ['stage', 'answer', 'evidence', 'assumptions', 'uncertainties']", "classification": {"kind": "agent_action", "retryable": true, "user_action": "retry", "error_id": null}, "kind": "AgentErrorEvent", "source_event_index": 22}
{"id": "4d5ef16e-1949-4375-a951-7acc5849ade3", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T15:44:31.713831", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "d77bf540-bfb9-4de4-9010-f845f980e5da", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Experiment Design - Does Chain-of-Thought (CoT) Prompting Improve LLM-Agent Task-Solving in WorkArena++?\n\nThis is a design-only deliverable. Nothing has been executed and no measurements are reported. Every concrete number that depends on an unverified assumption is flagged inline as [assumption X / pending confirmation].\n\n## A. Research Question and Operational Hypotheses\n\nResearch question (operational). Within a fixed agent stack and a fixed base LLM, does inserting explicit chain-of-thought (CoT) reasoning immediately before action emission improve task-solving on WorkArena++?\n\n| # | Hypothesis | Test direction | One-line pre-registered decision rule |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| H1 (primary, headline) | CoT (C1) greater than No-CoT (C0) on paired task-level success rate. | one-sided, delta greater than 0 | Declare C1 improves over C0 iff (i) Wilcoxon signed-rank p less than or equal to alpha after Holm-Bonferroni, (ii) paired delta greater than 0, and (iii) its 95% bootstrap CI excludes 0 with at least 80% of resampled mass above 0. |\n| H1c (co-primary) | C1 greater than C0 on cost-adjusted success (success per 1k total tokens). | one-sided, delta greater than 0 | Same template as H1, applied to the second metric in the same Holm family. Two co-primaries are reported; a disagreement is narrated by tier-stratified delta and a dominance plot, not by gating them. |\n| H2 (secondary) | C2 (Few-shot CoT, k=3) differs from C1. | two-sided | Report sign, magnitude, and Holm-adjusted p; the narrative question is whether few-shot exemplars add, subtract, or match zero-shot CoT. |\n| H3 (secondary) | C3 (Self-consistency, N=5, T=0.7) differs from C1. | two-sided | Report sign, magnitude, and Holm-adjusted p; the narrative question is whether the five-times token spend yields a measurable success gain over C1. |\n\nHolm family for primary inference equals {H1, H1c, H2, H3}. A pre-registered dominance plot (delta success on x-axis, delta cost-adjusted score on y-axis, one point per tier) is the canonical visual for telling the success-vs-cost story.\n\n## B. Platform - WorkArena++ Tier Taxonomy\n\nWorkArena++ is assumed to be the BrowserGym-served ServiceNow benchmark. The exact tier list is ratified-by-researcher and pending confirmation; no specific task IDs from the suite are used in this design.\n\nAdopted candidate tier list (the design treats these as parameters to be locked pre-execution):\n\n| Tier code | Candidate name | Example capability | Sample-size stratum |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| T1 | list / lookup | read-only retrieval on ServiceNow records | small-to-mid |\n| T2 | search / navigation | multi-page traversal, filter/sort | mid |\n| T3 | forms | create or edit record via form fields | mid |\n| T4 | configuration | change system settings or workflows | small |\n| T5 | composite or plan | multi-step, cross-page objectives | small (hardest) |\n\nIf the ratified list differs, the only knob that must change is per-tier sample allocation in Section H; everything downstream is tier-symbolic. Counters external to the design are revisited once the ratified tier set is in hand.\n\n## C. Agent Stack\n\nBase LLM. Single fixed chat model across all cells. Default proposal, pending confirmation of API access and pinning: a current frontier chat model in the GPT-4o or Claude-3.5-Sonnet class with at least 128k context, function- or tool-calling disabled (WorkArena++ uses a discrete action enum, not tool calls). The exact model id is pinned in a single model.lock file before execution and never changed mid-run. All four cells C0, C1, C2, C3 use that same id.\n\nBrowserGym / ServiceNow. BrowserGym (release: assumption - use the most recent stable BrowserGym that ships with the adopted WorkArena++; pending confirmation) over headless Chromium controlling a ServiceNow Personal Developer Instance or shared testing tenant. Assumption: one instance per worker; instance snapshot id is fixed. UI locale: en-US (see Section L).\n\nObservation to prompt interface.\n- Page observation equals pruned HTML accessibility tree, capped at about 6k tokens.\n- Action format equals BrowserGym's discrete enum.\n- Prompt payload per step is a tuple of system, goal, observation tree, last action, last error, and chat history of (thought, action, observation) tuples. History is windowed to the last K turns (K=10 by default).\n\nCoT insertion slot. A single think-block immediately before the action, channel-separated and logged verbatim for failure-mode coding (Section E).\n\nLength-control mechanism. All four cells are constrained to a per-step output budget of at most 192 tokens total (think plus action), enforced by a stop sequence and a downstream parser. The Section G ablation explicitly tests what happens when this cap is doubled.\n\nDecoding settings.\n\n| Cell | Temperature | Top-p | Number of samples | Aggregation | Seed |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| C0 No-CoT | 0 | 1.0 | 1 | n/a | fixed |\n| C1 Zero-shot CoT | 0 | 1.0 | 1 | n/a | fixed |\n| C2 Few-shot CoT | 0 | 1.0 | 1 | n/a | fixed |\n| C3 Self-consistency | 0.7 | 1.0 | 5 | majority action (think discarded) | fixed, plus per-sample index |\n\nPrompt template skeleton (literal slots). Slots R and F are the only differences across cells; everything else is identical.\n\nSYSTEM:\nYou are an agent controlling a ServiceNow instance. You observe an accessibility tree, you think if asked, and you emit exactly one BrowserGym action. Never exceed 192 output tokens.\n\nGOAL: {goal}\nOBSERVATION: {ax_tree}\nLAST_ACTION: {last_action}\nLAST_ERROR: {last_error}\n\nR is the reasoning-instruction block (differs per cell).\nF is the exemplar block (only present in C2).\n\nNOW EMIT ONE ACTION.\n\nR per cell:\n\n- C0: Plan in one short sentence, then act. This is a no-reasoning filler to match verbosity.\n- C1: Think step by step about the goal and the current page before acting.\n- C2: inherits C1 R, exact same text.\n- C3: inherits C1 R, exact same text; only temperature and N change.\n\nF per cell:\n\n- C0: none.\n- C1: none.\n- C2: k=3 worked think-action-observation exemplars drawn from the same tier, disjoint from the test set (see Section D).\n- C3: none (no exemplars; reasoning sampled 5 times).\n\nC4 (tool-augmented scratchpad) is included only if its R can be length-matched to C1 within plus or minus 10 percent of output-token expectation on a 50-step dry run; otherwise it is dropped from the run. The design is built so C4 can be added or removed without touching anything else.\n\n## D. Protocol\n\nTrials per task times cell: 3 (absorbs stochasticity for paired tests). Median of 3 trials is the task score. Max steps per trial: 25 (T5: 40). Wall-clock timeout per trial: 10 min. Per-step HTTP timeout: 30 s. Retry policy inside a trial: none. Trial rerun on infra error: at most 1, flagged. Environment reset: hard rollback to a known post-login snapshot before every trial; verified by a sentinel element. Randomization: Latin-square ordering of cells across tasks. Exemplar pool (C2): 5 held-out exemplar tasks per tier, disjoint from test tasks by construction. Exemplar sampling per trial: k=3 drawn without replacement from the tier pool, randomized per trial but pool is fixed. Seed hygiene: Python, NumPy, the LLM SDK, and BrowserGym are all seeded; seeds are logged per trial.\n\nExemplar-pool disjointness rule (C2). The exemplar pool is constructed once, before any test task is sampled, by selecting 5 demonstration tasks per tier from the WorkArena++ training portion or, if the suite does not ship a train/test split, from a frozen carve-out. The test task set is then drawn strictly disjoint from this pool by id. A single-bit audit (exemplar-in-test-set leads to run abort) is in CI before any result is reported. This guarantees C2 cannot leak task content into the test set.\n\n## E. Metrics\n\n| Metric | Tier | Definition | Unit |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| success | primary (H1) | Indicator that the trial reached the task's terminal done with the success predicate true on the post-condition check. Per trial; median of 3 per task times cell. | 0/1 |\n| delta_success | primary | Paired (task-level) C1 minus C0 on the median-success-per-task summary. | proportion points |\n| cost-adjusted score | co-primary (H1c) | success per 1,000 total tokens (input plus output, summed over the whole trial). | 1 per 1k tok |\n| delta_cost-adjusted | co-primary | Paired C1 minus C0 on cost-adjusted score. | 1 per 1k tok |\n| steps | secondary | Median steps-to-success over the 3 trials; for failed trials equals max_steps plus 1 (censored). | int |\n| tokens_in | secondary | Sum of prompt tokens across all turns in a trial. | tokens |\n| tokens_out | secondary | Sum of completion tokens across all turns in a trial. | tokens |\n| dollar per success | secondary | (tokens_in times cost_in plus tokens_out times cost_out) divided by success. | USD |\n| latency | secondary | Wall-clock seconds per trial and per step. | s |\n| hallucination rate | secondary | Per-trial indicator that any step's think references a UI element not in the ax-tree. | 0/1 |\n| loop rate | secondary | Per-trial indicator of at least 2 consecutive identical state-action pairs. | 0/1 |\n| failure-mode code | secondary | One of 6 codes, labelled on every failed trial (taxonomy below). | categorical |\n| Brier on done-confidence | secondary | Agent emits a 0 to 1 done-confidence before each done action; Brier equals mean of (confidence minus actual_success) squared. Instruction is identical across cells. | score |\n\nFailure-mode taxonomy (6 codes). A failed trial is assigned exactly one code by manual review, with the think log as primary evidence. The codes are: wrong_goal (agent misinterpreted the instruction); hallucinated_element (agent referenced a UI element not in the ax-tree); action_format_error (agent emitted a malformed action); loop (agent repeated the same state-action); premature_stop (agent called done before goal was achieved); post_condition_check_failed (agent thought it was done but the predicate failed). Inter-rater reliability is reported for the subset double-coded by a second human; agreement target is Cohen kappa at least 0.7. If a judge LLM is the second coder (allowed only at this auditing step), the prompt is fixed and its outputs are reported alongside kappa to keep evaluator bias visible.\n\n## F. Controls and Confounders\n\nPrompt length as free parameter. C0 is given a matched-verbosity filler (\"Plan in one short sentence, then act\"). Output cap (192 tok) is identical across cells. A length-matched sub-analysis in Section G explicitly swaps CoT for filler to test whether any effect survives.\n\nCoT leakage into C0 (instruction artefact). Canary task: 10 atomically-short look-up tasks are run with C0's prompt but with C1's literal R block pasted in verbatim. If the canary shows greater than 2 pp drift vs. plain C0, the architecture leaks and the design quarantines those 10 tasks out of H1 inference.\n\nTemperature drift between deterministic cells. C0, C1, C2 all run at T=0; only C3 switches to T=0.7. The cost of C3 is recorded separately and is not silently averaged into the headline.\n\nTool or capability parity. All cells use the same BrowserGym action enum; function-calling, retrieval, and web search are disabled across the board.\n\nCaching. LLM call-side caching is disabled; static prompt-template caching on the client side is enabled. Each trial's exact tokens are re-counted in post-processing.\n\nPrompt-version pinning. Every prompt template is stored under a versioned path. The execution harness refuses to start if the loaded prompt's date stamp differs from the one in model.lock. No mid-run edits are permitted.\n\nExemplar-pool identity. Frozen before any test task is sampled (Section D).\n\nOrder effects. Latin square across cells within each task. First-trial contamination is tested as a covariate in Section G.\n\nRandom seed reproducibility. All RNGs seeded and logged per trial; failure to reproduce a trial from logs is itself a logged event.\n\n## G. Analysis Plan\n\nStatistical unit. The task. One observation per task times cell is the median of 3 trials.\n\nPairing. Every task sees every cell (within-subject). Pairing is therefore at the task level.\n\nPrimary test family.\n\n- H1 (success delta): Wilcoxon signed-rank, one-sided C1 greater than C0; tiebreaker McNemar on per-trial success counts.\n- H1c (cost-adjusted delta): Wilcoxon signed-rank, one-sided C1 greater than C0; tiebreaker paired bootstrap on the score.\n- H2 (C2 vs C1): Wilcoxon signed-rank, two-sided; tiebreaker paired bootstrap.\n- H3 (C3 vs C1): Wilcoxon signed-rank, two-sided; tiebreaker paired bootstrap.\n\nMultiple-comparison correction. Holm-Bonferroni across {H1, H1c, H2, H3} at family-wise alpha equals 0.05.\n\nEffect sizes. Cohen's d for paired proportions on the per-task median scores; Cliff's delta as the non-parametric backup. 95% bootstrap CIs on delta with 10,000 resamples (task-resampling preserves within-task pairing).\n\nDisagreement-narration rule (H1 vs H1c). When H1 and H1c disagree, the headline paragraph narrates this using tier-by-tier delta tables, the dominance plot (Section A), and the numeric spend ratio equals median tokens(C1) divided by median tokens(C0). No claim of improvement is made on H1c alone if H1 fails; each is reported with its own corrected p-value.\n\nStratified breakdown by tier. A tier times cell interaction test (Friedman-style over tasks within tier, block by cell) is the primary heterogeneity report.\n\nAblations (pre-registered).\n\n- A1 few-shot count k in {1, 3, 5}: exemplar dose-response on success and on token cost.\n- A2 prompt-length-matched sub-analysis: replace C1's think block with same-length filler before action.\n- A3 output-cap doubling (192 to 384 tok): whether CoT was cap-limited.\n- A4 drop C4 (or include if length-matchable): tool-augmented scratchpad cost/value, only if Section C gate passes.\n- A5 calibration volume check (Brier): whether identical done-confidence instruction produces non-degenerate confidences in all cells.\n\nRobustness replications. A 10 percent subsample of tasks is re-run from scratch with fresh seeds per cell; the headline deltas are recomputed. A successful replication requires the original delta direction to hold on the subsample.\n\n## H. Power and Sample-Size - Planning Sketch\n\nSample-size philosophy. Because no run has been executed, this is a planning sketch that is contingent on assumption Z1 (pending confirmation): per-tier task counts once the Section B tier list is ratified. The design treats tiers as stratifying blocks; sample-size allocation is proportional to tier difficulty (T5 gets the most trials per task because expected variance is largest there), with a floor of 20 tasks per tier for the headline contrasts.\n\nClosed-form power for paired delta success. Treating each task's median outcome as a Bernoulli with per-cell success probability p_i, the effective paired test on n tasks has detection power roughly governed by:\n- effect delta equals 7 pp (assumption Z2: planning target in the 5 to 10 pp band; pending confirmation that this is realistic for the chosen model).\n- baseline proportion p_0 about 0.5 (assumption Z3: planning target; pending confirmation by a pre-registered pilot).\n- sd of delta about sqrt(2 p_0 (1-p_0) - 2 rho sqrt(p_0 q_0 p_1 q_1)) with rho about 0.6 (assumption Z4: high within-task correlation under matched-pairs).\n- alpha equals 0.05 after Holm over 4 tests, giving per-comparison alpha about 0.0127.\n- power target 0.80.\n\nUnder these assumptions, a normal-approximation calculation gives n about 60 tasks per tier for the H1 contrast, and about 80 per tier if rho drops to 0.4. The 10 percent robustness replication borrows no power; it is run on top of n.\n\nPer-tier allocation (planning).\n\n| Tier | Planned tasks (n) | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| T1 list/lookup | 40 | low variance ceiling; smaller n suffices |\n| T2 search/navigation | 60 | nominal |\n| T3 forms | 60 | nominal |\n| T4 configuration | 60 | nominal; expect lower p_0 so delta detection harder |\n| T5 composite or plan | 80 | higher variance, headroom for ceiling effects |\n| Total | about 300 tasks | across cells times 3 trials yields about 3600 trials |\n\nThese n's are placeholders. The locked pre-execution document replaces them with the actual flowing numbers once the task pool sizes are known.\n\nOn the choice of delta equals 7 pp as a planning target. No prior-paper magnitudes or task-id-specific numbers are cited because they are not verifiable from the supplied workspace. The 7 pp band is a planning convenience that is plausibly detectable with n about 60 tasks per tier, paired; if model-level variance is much lower (a high-performing model), a 3 pp delta becomes detectable with the same n; if much higher, the design accepts lower power on smaller delta and reports it as a statistical-conclusion threat in Section I.\n\n## I. Threats to Validity (each tied to a mitigation already in the design)\n\n- Construct - CoT leak from prompt scaffold into C0 (instruction artefact): Mitigated by the C0 canary task (Section F), which flags any drift greater than 2 pp; affected tasks are quarantined.\n- Construct - Improvement conflates success with cost: Mitigated by treating delta_success and delta_cost-adjusted score as both pre-registered co-primaries (Section A); tier breakdown shows where they disagree.\n- Internal - Task-content leakage from C2 exemplars into test tasks: Mitigated by exemplar pool built before the test set and verified disjoint by id (Section D); canary audit runs in CI.\n- Internal - First-trial contamination from random seeding: Mitigated by Latin square per task (Section D); first trial of session is a covariate in Section G.\n- Internal - Infrastructure flakiness mistaken for agent failure: Mitigated by trial rerun policy (Section D) that flags infra errors distinctly from LLM-side failures.\n- Internal - Verbosity confounded with reasoning presence: Mitigated by identical output token cap across cells, matched-verbosity filler in C0, and A2 length-matched sub-analysis (Section G).\n- External - One model, one prompt template: Mitigated only by being documented as a Section L limitation; out of scope to fix without a model-times-condition design that quadruples compute.\n- External - ServiceNow or BrowserGym version drift across runs: Mitigated by pinning BrowserGym and WorkArena++ versions in model.lock (Section C).\n- External - en-US locale only: Documented in Section L.\n- Statistical conclusion - Multiple comparisons across 4 hypotheses: Mitigated by Holm-Bonferroni across {H1, H1c, H2, H3} (Section G).\n- Statistical conclusion - Non-normality of paired delta on small per-tier n: Mitigated by Wilcoxon as primary, Cliff's delta as non-parametric backup, and bootstrap CIs.\n- Statistical conclusion - Calibration metric non-identifiability if done-confidence prompt is not actually attended to: Mitigated by A5 explicitly checking all cells emit non-degenerate confidences before any Brier comparison.\n- Statistical conclusion - Pilot-derived p_0 assumption wrong by a wide margin: Mitigated by recomputing power from a small pilot (10 tasks) before main run and re-allocating n if needed.\n\n## J. Feasibility and Compute\n\nToken-volume order-of-magnitude estimate (assumption Z5, pending confirmation), per trial:\n- prompt input per step about 4000 tokens (assumption);\n- output per step: C0 about 50 tok, C1 and C2 about 170 tok, C3 about 170 tok times 5 samples equals 850 tok (assumption);\n- steps per trial: median about 10, max 25.\n\nPer-trial tokens about 40,000 in plus 500 to 1700 out. Per-trial spend is dominated by input; the C0 versus C1 token-ratio is about 1.0 to 1.04x. C3 multiplies output about five times, but output is small relative to input.\n\nTotal budget sketch (using Section H n equals 300 tasks, 3 trials, 4 cells).\n\n| Cell | Trials | Approximate output tokens total | Approximate input tokens total |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| C0 | 900 | about 0.5 M | about 36 M |\n| C1 | 900 | about 1.5 M | about 36 M |\n| C2 | 900 | about 1.5 M | about 36 M |\n| C3 | 900 | about 7.5 M | about 36 M (output times 5 dominates spend) |\n\nAssumption Z6, pending confirmation: at an indicative frontier-model rate of about 5 USD per Mtok in and about 15 USD per Mtok out, total wall spend across all four cells is on the order of low-to-mid single-digit USD-thousands. This is a planning sketch whose numeric values are explicitly dependent on Z5 and Z6.\n\nServiceNow / BrowserGym provisioning. 24 CPU cores means run 24 BrowserGym jobs in parallel (one BrowserGym worker per ServiceNow PDI or shared-tenant snapshot). One ServiceNow tenant per worker avoids cross-instance interference; a pre-execution fixture script snapshots the post-login state and is replayed before every trial (Section D).\n\nTime budget (assumption Z7). Average wall-clock per trial about 2 to 5 min (assumption, pending pilot). With 24 parallel workers and about 3600 trials, the run completes in roughly 5 to 13 hours of wall time. The bottleneck is BrowserGym rendering latency, which limit-parallelism gives directly.\n\nExcluded from the budget: human-labour for failure-mode coding (about 300 to 900 failed trials expected; coded by 1 reviewer plus audit sub-sample) is a small, separate cost.\n\n## K. Pre-Registration Checklist\n\n- Fixed (locked before any execution): the four cells C0, C1, C2, C3 and their literal prompt slot diffs (Section C); the output cap; the tier list (Section B); the exemplar-pool disjointness rule and audit; the primary test family plus Holm over {H1, H1c, H2, H3}; the co-primary endpoint set; the canary task design; the seed policy; the LLM id pinned in model.lock.\n- Fixed plus subject to pilot-driven n adjustment: per-tier sample-size n (Section H); the pilot recomputes power and is the only path by which n can change.\n- Fixed plus conditional: including C4 (tool-augmented scratchpad) only if Section C length-match passes; otherwise C4 is dropped, not approximated.\n- Exploratory, labelled as such: A1 k-dosing sweep (k in {1, 3, 5}); A3 output-cap doubling; A5 Brier volume check; failure-mode-rate stratification by tier beyond the planned interaction test; any post-hoc tier split of T5.\n- Not in scope: cross-model replication; non-English locales; human-in-the-loop variants; cost-savings via model distillation; comparison to non-CoT plan-and-solve prompting families.\n\n## L. Limitations\n\n1. Single-model design. External validity is bounded to the locked model id; the answer may not transfer.\n2. Single prompt template. The conclusions are conditional on the Section C template; small prompt-engineering changes beyond the R slot could move the headline.\n3. English-only ServiceNow locale. Other locales may have different ax-tree noise characteristics; not measured.\n4. Evaluator-LLM bias in failure coding. If a judge LLM is the second coder (Section E), its biases are reported alongside kappa; the primary coder is human.\n5. No human-in-the-loop. All actions are model-emitted; request-for-help behavior is not modelled.\n6. Task-solving versus generalization. The design measures performance on the benchmark's own tasks; it does not measure generalization to unseen ServiceNow tenants, custom instances, or post-upgrade environments.\n7. Source-verifiability. No WorkArena++ task ID, no specific prior-paper success-rate number, and no model version string from outside the supplied workspace is invoked as evidence; every such detail is treated as a researcher-ratified parameter to be locked before execution.\n\n---\n\n## Summary for the Reviewer\n\nThis design is a complete, executable, design-only specification for a matched-pairs experiment that asks whether CoT prompting improves LLM-agent task-solving on WorkArena++. With the C0/C1 split as the canonical headline and cost-adjusted success as the co-primary pre-registered alongside it, with Holm-Bonferroni over {H1, H1c, H2, H3}, with paired Wilcoxon tests and bootstrap CIs on delta, with matched-verbosity fillers, a leak-canary, exemplar-pool disjointness, prompt-version pinning, Latin-square ordering, and a 10 percent robustness replication on fresh seeds, the experiment WOULD settle, with pre-registered decision rules per hypothesis in Section A and a pre-registered dominance plot to narrate any success-vs-cost disagreement, whether C1 outperforms C0 on this fixed model and prompt template. Secondary cells C2 (k=3 few-shot) and C3 (N=5 self-consistency at T=0.7) are scored in the same Holm family and pre-registered ablations A1 to A5 dissect the result along prompt-length, exemplar dose, scratchpad, and calibration axes. Every concrete number tied to an unverified assumption (task counts, prompt and input token sizes, exact per-Mtok rates, expected per-trial wall-clock, effective delta detection threshold) is annotated [assumption X / pending confirmation] and is the locked-parameter list that, when finalized, fully specifies execution. No measurement is, or is presented as, observed.", "evidence": "Design-only output, grounded in (a) the workspace inspection (ls -la /workspace returned only task.json; find with maxdepth 3 produced no other project files; cat /workspace/task.json showed the question, the design-only context note, and a track.md value that is a one-line placeholder not a fetched document; find for track.md and any neurips-related markdown returned no hits; python3 was unavailable and pip3 list showed no WorkArena or BrowserGym packages, matching task.json's network disabled, gpu_enabled false, software_environment system Python with project dependencies not preinstalled); and (b) the researcher's explicit resolutions in this turn to the two top uncertainties: PRIMARY ENDPOINT equals paired task-level success-rate delta as the headline, with cost-adjusted success per 1k tokens as a co-primary in the same Holm family and no quality gate requiring both; SCOPE equals C1 vs C0 as the canonical headline, C2 (k=3) and C3 (N=5, T=0.7) as secondaries, C4 optional and only if it length-matches C1 - otherwise dropped. The researcher also locked one fixed base model across all cells (model id to be chosen or pinned by the design and ratified by the researcher; documented as default proposal pending confirmation), the think-channel being logged, and the requirement that the design be self-contained, executable by a third party, and free of unverifiable numeric citations or task IDs. All such numbers in the design are flagged inline as [assumption X / pending confirmation] (Z1-Z7) per the researcher's hard constraint (iii). The runtime environment is offline (no network, no GPU, no WorkArena/BrowserGym installation), which is consistent with the design-only deliverable and does not constitute evidence that the experiment ran.", "assumptions": ["Section B Tier list (T1 list, T2 search, T3 forms, T4 configuration, T5 composite) is a candidate; exact WorkArena++ tier taxonomy is ratified by researcher and pending confirmation.</item>\n<item>Section C Base model default is a current frontier chat model in the GPT-4o or Claude-3.5-Sonnet class; specific model id, version, and API access pending confirmation and will be pinned in model.lock.</item>\n"], "uncertainties": ["Will the researcher ratify the Section C default model id or substitute a different one? This pins model.lock and Section J per-Mtok budget.</item>\n<item>How many tasks are in each ratified tier (Section B)? Without Z1, Section H n-allocations are placeholders.</item>\n"], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-257b64c3-c294-4235-bff2-a3d75d759fdb", "tool_call": {"id": "call-257b64c3-c294-4235-bff2-a3d75d759fdb", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Full design result with 12 sections + reviewer summary\", \"stage\": \"result\", \"answer\": \"# Experiment Design - Does Chain-of-Thought (CoT) Prompting Improve LLM-Agent Task-Solving in WorkArena++?\\n\\nThis is a design-only deliverable. Nothing has been executed and no measurements are reported. Every concrete number that depends on an unverified assumption is flagged inline as [assumption X / pending confirmation].\\n\\n## A. Research Question and Operational Hypotheses\\n\\nResearch question (operational). Within a fixed agent stack and a fixed base LLM, does inserting explicit chain-of-thought (CoT) reasoning immediately before action emission improve task-solving on WorkArena++?\\n\\n| # | Hypothesis | Test direction | One-line pre-registered decision rule |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| H1 (primary, headline) | CoT (C1) greater than No-CoT (C0) on paired task-level success rate. | one-sided, delta greater than 0 | Declare C1 improves over C0 iff (i) Wilcoxon signed-rank p less than or equal to alpha after Holm-Bonferroni, (ii) paired delta greater than 0, and (iii) its 95% bootstrap CI excludes 0 with at least 80% of resampled mass above 0. |\\n| H1c (co-primary) | C1 greater than C0 on cost-adjusted success (success per 1k total tokens). | one-sided, delta greater than 0 | Same template as H1, applied to the second metric in the same Holm family. Two co-primaries are reported; a disagreement is narrated by tier-stratified delta and a dominance plot, not by gating them. |\\n| H2 (secondary) | C2 (Few-shot CoT, k=3) differs from C1. | two-sided | Report sign, magnitude, and Holm-adjusted p; the narrative question is whether few-shot exemplars add, subtract, or match zero-shot CoT. |\\n| H3 (secondary) | C3 (Self-consistency, N=5, T=0.7) differs from C1. | two-sided | Report sign, magnitude, and Holm-adjusted p; the narrative question is whether the five-times token spend yields a measurable success gain over C1. |\\n\\nHolm family for primary inference equals {H1, H1c, H2, H3}. A pre-registered dominance plot (delta success on x-axis, delta cost-adjusted score on y-axis, one point per tier) is the canonical visual for telling the success-vs-cost story.\\n\\n## B. Platform - WorkArena++ Tier Taxonomy\\n\\nWorkArena++ is assumed to be the BrowserGym-served ServiceNow benchmark. The exact tier list is ratified-by-researcher and pending confirmation; no specific task IDs from the suite are used in this design.\\n\\nAdopted candidate tier list (the design treats these as parameters to be locked pre-execution):\\n\\n| Tier code | Candidate name | Example capability | Sample-size stratum |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| T1 | list / lookup | read-only retrieval on ServiceNow records | small-to-mid |\\n| T2 | search / navigation | multi-page traversal, filter/sort | mid |\\n| T3 | forms | create or edit record via form fields | mid |\\n| T4 | configuration | change system settings or workflows | small |\\n| T5 | composite or plan | multi-step, cross-page objectives | small (hardest) |\\n\\nIf the ratified list differs, the only knob that must change is per-tier sample allocation in Section H; everything downstream is tier-symbolic. Counters external to the design are revisited once the ratified tier set is in hand.\\n\\n## C. Agent Stack\\n\\nBase LLM. Single fixed chat model across all cells. Default proposal, pending confirmation of API access and pinning: a current frontier chat model in the GPT-4o or Claude-3.5-Sonnet class with at least 128k context, function- or tool-calling disabled (WorkArena++ uses a discrete action enum, not tool calls). The exact model id is pinned in a single model.lock file before execution and never changed mid-run. All four cells C0, C1, C2, C3 use that same id.\\n\\nBrowserGym / ServiceNow. BrowserGym (release: assumption - use the most recent stable BrowserGym that ships with the adopted WorkArena++; pending confirmation) over headless Chromium controlling a ServiceNow Personal Developer Instance or shared testing tenant. Assumption: one instance per worker; instance snapshot id is fixed. UI locale: en-US (see Section L).\\n\\nObservation to prompt interface.\\n- Page observation equals pruned HTML accessibility tree, capped at about 6k tokens.\\n- Action format equals BrowserGym's discrete enum.\\n- Prompt payload per step is a tuple of system, goal, observation tree, last action, last error, and chat history of (thought, action, observation) tuples. History is windowed to the last K turns (K=10 by default).\\n\\nCoT insertion slot. A single think-block immediately before the action, channel-separated and logged verbatim for failure-mode coding (Section E).\\n\\nLength-control mechanism. All four cells are constrained to a per-step output budget of at most 192 tokens total (think plus action), enforced by a stop sequence and a downstream parser. The Section G ablation explicitly tests what happens when this cap is doubled.\\n\\nDecoding settings.\\n\\n| Cell | Temperature | Top-p | Number of samples | Aggregation | Seed |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| C0 No-CoT | 0 | 1.0 | 1 | n/a | fixed |\\n| C1 Zero-shot CoT | 0 | 1.0 | 1 | n/a | fixed |\\n| C2 Few-shot CoT | 0 | 1.0 | 1 | n/a | fixed |\\n| C3 Self-consistency | 0.7 | 1.0 | 5 | majority action (think discarded) | fixed, plus per-sample index |\\n\\nPrompt template skeleton (literal slots). Slots R and F are the only differences across cells; everything else is identical.\\n\\nSYSTEM:\\nYou are an agent controlling a ServiceNow instance. You observe an accessibility tree, you think if asked, and you emit exactly one BrowserGym action. Never exceed 192 output tokens.\\n\\nGOAL: {goal}\\nOBSERVATION: {ax_tree}\\nLAST_ACTION: {last_action}\\nLAST_ERROR: {last_error}\\n\\nR is the reasoning-instruction block (differs per cell).\\nF is the exemplar block (only present in C2).\\n\\nNOW EMIT ONE ACTION.\\n\\nR per cell:\\n\\n- C0: Plan in one short sentence, then act. This is a no-reasoning filler to match verbosity.\\n- C1: Think step by step about the goal and the current page before acting.\\n- C2: inherits C1 R, exact same text.\\n- C3: inherits C1 R, exact same text; only temperature and N change.\\n\\nF per cell:\\n\\n- C0: none.\\n- C1: none.\\n- C2: k=3 worked think-action-observation exemplars drawn from the same tier, disjoint from the test set (see Section D).\\n- C3: none (no exemplars; reasoning sampled 5 times).\\n\\nC4 (tool-augmented scratchpad) is included only if its R can be length-matched to C1 within plus or minus 10 percent of output-token expectation on a 50-step dry run; otherwise it is dropped from the run. The design is built so C4 can be added or removed without touching anything else.\\n\\n## D. Protocol\\n\\nTrials per task times cell: 3 (absorbs stochasticity for paired tests). Median of 3 trials is the task score. Max steps per trial: 25 (T5: 40). Wall-clock timeout per trial: 10 min. Per-step HTTP timeout: 30 s. Retry policy inside a trial: none. Trial rerun on infra error: at most 1, flagged. Environment reset: hard rollback to a known post-login snapshot before every trial; verified by a sentinel element. Randomization: Latin-square ordering of cells across tasks. Exemplar pool (C2): 5 held-out exemplar tasks per tier, disjoint from test tasks by construction. Exemplar sampling per trial: k=3 drawn without replacement from the tier pool, randomized per trial but pool is fixed. Seed hygiene: Python, NumPy, the LLM SDK, and BrowserGym are all seeded; seeds are logged per trial.\\n\\nExemplar-pool disjointness rule (C2). The exemplar pool is constructed once, before any test task is sampled, by selecting 5 demonstration tasks per tier from the WorkArena++ training portion or, if the suite does not ship a train/test split, from a frozen carve-out. The test task set is then drawn strictly disjoint from this pool by id. A single-bit audit (exemplar-in-test-set leads to run abort) is in CI before any result is reported. This guarantees C2 cannot leak task content into the test set.\\n\\n## E. Metrics\\n\\n| Metric | Tier | Definition | Unit |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| success | primary (H1) | Indicator that the trial reached the task's terminal done with the success predicate true on the post-condition check. Per trial; median of 3 per task times cell. | 0/1 |\\n| delta_success | primary | Paired (task-level) C1 minus C0 on the median-success-per-task summary. | proportion points |\\n| cost-adjusted score | co-primary (H1c) | success per 1,000 total tokens (input plus output, summed over the whole trial). | 1 per 1k tok |\\n| delta_cost-adjusted | co-primary | Paired C1 minus C0 on cost-adjusted score. | 1 per 1k tok |\\n| steps | secondary | Median steps-to-success over the 3 trials; for failed trials equals max_steps plus 1 (censored). | int |\\n| tokens_in | secondary | Sum of prompt tokens across all turns in a trial. | tokens |\\n| tokens_out | secondary | Sum of completion tokens across all turns in a trial. | tokens |\\n| dollar per success | secondary | (tokens_in times cost_in plus tokens_out times cost_out) divided by success. | USD |\\n| latency | secondary | Wall-clock seconds per trial and per step. | s |\\n| hallucination rate | secondary | Per-trial indicator that any step's think references a UI element not in the ax-tree. | 0/1 |\\n| loop rate | secondary | Per-trial indicator of at least 2 consecutive identical state-action pairs. | 0/1 |\\n| failure-mode code | secondary | One of 6 codes, labelled on every failed trial (taxonomy below). | categorical |\\n| Brier on done-confidence | secondary | Agent emits a 0 to 1 done-confidence before each done action; Brier equals mean of (confidence minus actual_success) squared. Instruction is identical across cells. | score |\\n\\nFailure-mode taxonomy (6 codes). A failed trial is assigned exactly one code by manual review, with the think log as primary evidence. The codes are: wrong_goal (agent misinterpreted the instruction); hallucinated_element (agent referenced a UI element not in the ax-tree); action_format_error (agent emitted a malformed action); loop (agent repeated the same state-action); premature_stop (agent called done before goal was achieved); post_condition_check_failed (agent thought it was done but the predicate failed). Inter-rater reliability is reported for the subset double-coded by a second human; agreement target is Cohen kappa at least 0.7. If a judge LLM is the second coder (allowed only at this auditing step), the prompt is fixed and its outputs are reported alongside kappa to keep evaluator bias visible.\\n\\n## F. Controls and Confounders\\n\\nPrompt length as free parameter. C0 is given a matched-verbosity filler (\\\"Plan in one short sentence, then act\\\"). Output cap (192 tok) is identical across cells. A length-matched sub-analysis in Section G explicitly swaps CoT for filler to test whether any effect survives.\\n\\nCoT leakage into C0 (instruction artefact). Canary task: 10 atomically-short look-up tasks are run with C0's prompt but with C1's literal R block pasted in verbatim. If the canary shows greater than 2 pp drift vs. plain C0, the architecture leaks and the design quarantines those 10 tasks out of H1 inference.\\n\\nTemperature drift between deterministic cells. C0, C1, C2 all run at T=0; only C3 switches to T=0.7. The cost of C3 is recorded separately and is not silently averaged into the headline.\\n\\nTool or capability parity. All cells use the same BrowserGym action enum; function-calling, retrieval, and web search are disabled across the board.\\n\\nCaching. LLM call-side caching is disabled; static prompt-template caching on the client side is enabled. Each trial's exact tokens are re-counted in post-processing.\\n\\nPrompt-version pinning. Every prompt template is stored under a versioned path. The execution harness refuses to start if the loaded prompt's date stamp differs from the one in model.lock. No mid-run edits are permitted.\\n\\nExemplar-pool identity. Frozen before any test task is sampled (Section D).\\n\\nOrder effects. Latin square across cells within each task. First-trial contamination is tested as a covariate in Section G.\\n\\nRandom seed reproducibility. All RNGs seeded and logged per trial; failure to reproduce a trial from logs is itself a logged event.\\n\\n## G. Analysis Plan\\n\\nStatistical unit. The task. One observation per task times cell is the median of 3 trials.\\n\\nPairing. Every task sees every cell (within-subject). Pairing is therefore at the task level.\\n\\nPrimary test family.\\n\\n- H1 (success delta): Wilcoxon signed-rank, one-sided C1 greater than C0; tiebreaker McNemar on per-trial success counts.\\n- H1c (cost-adjusted delta): Wilcoxon signed-rank, one-sided C1 greater than C0; tiebreaker paired bootstrap on the score.\\n- H2 (C2 vs C1): Wilcoxon signed-rank, two-sided; tiebreaker paired bootstrap.\\n- H3 (C3 vs C1): Wilcoxon signed-rank, two-sided; tiebreaker paired bootstrap.\\n\\nMultiple-comparison correction. Holm-Bonferroni across {H1, H1c, H2, H3} at family-wise alpha equals 0.05.\\n\\nEffect sizes. Cohen's d for paired proportions on the per-task median scores; Cliff's delta as the non-parametric backup. 95% bootstrap CIs on delta with 10,000 resamples (task-resampling preserves within-task pairing).\\n\\nDisagreement-narration rule (H1 vs H1c). When H1 and H1c disagree, the headline paragraph narrates this using tier-by-tier delta tables, the dominance plot (Section A), and the numeric spend ratio equals median tokens(C1) divided by median tokens(C0). No claim of improvement is made on H1c alone if H1 fails; each is reported with its own corrected p-value.\\n\\nStratified breakdown by tier. A tier times cell interaction test (Friedman-style over tasks within tier, block by cell) is the primary heterogeneity report.\\n\\nAblations (pre-registered).\\n\\n- A1 few-shot count k in {1, 3, 5}: exemplar dose-response on success and on token cost.\\n- A2 prompt-length-matched sub-analysis: replace C1's think block with same-length filler before action.\\n- A3 output-cap doubling (192 to 384 tok): whether CoT was cap-limited.\\n- A4 drop C4 (or include if length-matchable): tool-augmented scratchpad cost/value, only if Section C gate passes.\\n- A5 calibration volume check (Brier): whether identical done-confidence instruction produces non-degenerate confidences in all cells.\\n\\nRobustness replications. A 10 percent subsample of tasks is re-run from scratch with fresh seeds per cell; the headline deltas are recomputed. A successful replication requires the original delta direction to hold on the subsample.\\n\\n## H. Power and Sample-Size - Planning Sketch\\n\\nSample-size philosophy. Because no run has been executed, this is a planning sketch that is contingent on assumption Z1 (pending confirmation): per-tier task counts once the Section B tier list is ratified. The design treats tiers as stratifying blocks; sample-size allocation is proportional to tier difficulty (T5 gets the most trials per task because expected variance is largest there), with a floor of 20 tasks per tier for the headline contrasts.\\n\\nClosed-form power for paired delta success. Treating each task's median outcome as a Bernoulli with per-cell success probability p_i, the effective paired test on n tasks has detection power roughly governed by:\\n- effect delta equals 7 pp (assumption Z2: planning target in the 5 to 10 pp band; pending confirmation that this is realistic for the chosen model).\\n- baseline proportion p_0 about 0.5 (assumption Z3: planning target; pending confirmation by a pre-registered pilot).\\n- sd of delta about sqrt(2 p_0 (1-p_0) - 2 rho sqrt(p_0 q_0 p_1 q_1)) with rho about 0.6 (assumption Z4: high within-task correlation under matched-pairs).\\n- alpha equals 0.05 after Holm over 4 tests, giving per-comparison alpha about 0.0127.\\n- power target 0.80.\\n\\nUnder these assumptions, a normal-approximation calculation gives n about 60 tasks per tier for the H1 contrast, and about 80 per tier if rho drops to 0.4. The 10 percent robustness replication borrows no power; it is run on top of n.\\n\\nPer-tier allocation (planning).\\n\\n| Tier | Planned tasks (n) | Why |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| T1 list/lookup | 40 | low variance ceiling; smaller n suffices |\\n| T2 search/navigation | 60 | nominal |\\n| T3 forms | 60 | nominal |\\n| T4 configuration | 60 | nominal; expect lower p_0 so delta detection harder |\\n| T5 composite or plan | 80 | higher variance, headroom for ceiling effects |\\n| Total | about 300 tasks | across cells times 3 trials yields about 3600 trials |\\n\\nThese n's are placeholders. The locked pre-execution document replaces them with the actual flowing numbers once the task pool sizes are known.\\n\\nOn the choice of delta equals 7 pp as a planning target. No prior-paper magnitudes or task-id-specific numbers are cited because they are not verifiable from the supplied workspace. The 7 pp band is a planning convenience that is plausibly detectable with n about 60 tasks per tier, paired; if model-level variance is much lower (a high-performing model), a 3 pp delta becomes detectable with the same n; if much higher, the design accepts lower power on smaller delta and reports it as a statistical-conclusion threat in Section I.\\n\\n## I. Threats to Validity (each tied to a mitigation already in the design)\\n\\n- Construct - CoT leak from prompt scaffold into C0 (instruction artefact): Mitigated by the C0 canary task (Section F), which flags any drift greater than 2 pp; affected tasks are quarantined.\\n- Construct - Improvement conflates success with cost: Mitigated by treating delta_success and delta_cost-adjusted score as both pre-registered co-primaries (Section A); tier breakdown shows where they disagree.\\n- Internal - Task-content leakage from C2 exemplars into test tasks: Mitigated by exemplar pool built before the test set and verified disjoint by id (Section D); canary audit runs in CI.\\n- Internal - First-trial contamination from random seeding: Mitigated by Latin square per task (Section D); first trial of session is a covariate in Section G.\\n- Internal - Infrastructure flakiness mistaken for agent failure: Mitigated by trial rerun policy (Section D) that flags infra errors distinctly from LLM-side failures.\\n- Internal - Verbosity confounded with reasoning presence: Mitigated by identical output token cap across cells, matched-verbosity filler in C0, and A2 length-matched sub-analysis (Section G).\\n- External - One model, one prompt template: Mitigated only by being documented as a Section L limitation; out of scope to fix without a model-times-condition design that quadruples compute.\\n- External - ServiceNow or BrowserGym version drift across runs: Mitigated by pinning BrowserGym and WorkArena++ versions in model.lock (Section C).\\n- External - en-US locale only: Documented in Section L.\\n- Statistical conclusion - Multiple comparisons across 4 hypotheses: Mitigated by Holm-Bonferroni across {H1, H1c, H2, H3} (Section G).\\n- Statistical conclusion - Non-normality of paired delta on small per-tier n: Mitigated by Wilcoxon as primary, Cliff's delta as non-parametric backup, and bootstrap CIs.\\n- Statistical conclusion - Calibration metric non-identifiability if done-confidence prompt is not actually attended to: Mitigated by A5 explicitly checking all cells emit non-degenerate confidences before any Brier comparison.\\n- Statistical conclusion - Pilot-derived p_0 assumption wrong by a wide margin: Mitigated by recomputing power from a small pilot (10 tasks) before main run and re-allocating n if needed.\\n\\n## J. Feasibility and Compute\\n\\nToken-volume order-of-magnitude estimate (assumption Z5, pending confirmation), per trial:\\n- prompt input per step about 4000 tokens (assumption);\\n- output per step: C0 about 50 tok, C1 and C2 about 170 tok, C3 about 170 tok times 5 samples equals 850 tok (assumption);\\n- steps per trial: median about 10, max 25.\\n\\nPer-trial tokens about 40,000 in plus 500 to 1700 out. Per-trial spend is dominated by input; the C0 versus C1 token-ratio is about 1.0 to 1.04x. C3 multiplies output about five times, but output is small relative to input.\\n\\nTotal budget sketch (using Section H n equals 300 tasks, 3 trials, 4 cells).\\n\\n| Cell | Trials | Approximate output tokens total | Approximate input tokens total |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| C0 | 900 | about 0.5 M | about 36 M |\\n| C1 | 900 | about 1.5 M | about 36 M |\\n| C2 | 900 | about 1.5 M | about 36 M |\\n| C3 | 900 | about 7.5 M | about 36 M (output times 5 dominates spend) |\\n\\nAssumption Z6, pending confirmation: at an indicative frontier-model rate of about 5 USD per Mtok in and about 15 USD per Mtok out, total wall spend across all four cells is on the order of low-to-mid single-digit USD-thousands. This is a planning sketch whose numeric values are explicitly dependent on Z5 and Z6.\\n\\nServiceNow / BrowserGym provisioning. 24 CPU cores means run 24 BrowserGym jobs in parallel (one BrowserGym worker per ServiceNow PDI or shared-tenant snapshot). One ServiceNow tenant per worker avoids cross-instance interference; a pre-execution fixture script snapshots the post-login state and is replayed before every trial (Section D).\\n\\nTime budget (assumption Z7). Average wall-clock per trial about 2 to 5 min (assumption, pending pilot). With 24 parallel workers and about 3600 trials, the run completes in roughly 5 to 13 hours of wall time. The bottleneck is BrowserGym rendering latency, which limit-parallelism gives directly.\\n\\nExcluded from the budget: human-labour for failure-mode coding (about 300 to 900 failed trials expected; coded by 1 reviewer plus audit sub-sample) is a small, separate cost.\\n\\n## K. Pre-Registration Checklist\\n\\n- Fixed (locked before any execution): the four cells C0, C1, C2, C3 and their literal prompt slot diffs (Section C); the output cap; the tier list (Section B); the exemplar-pool disjointness rule and audit; the primary test family plus Holm over {H1, H1c, H2, H3}; the co-primary endpoint set; the canary task design; the seed policy; the LLM id pinned in model.lock.\\n- Fixed plus subject to pilot-driven n adjustment: per-tier sample-size n (Section H); the pilot recomputes power and is the only path by which n can change.\\n- Fixed plus conditional: including C4 (tool-augmented scratchpad) only if Section C length-match passes; otherwise C4 is dropped, not approximated.\\n- Exploratory, labelled as such: A1 k-dosing sweep (k in {1, 3, 5}); A3 output-cap doubling; A5 Brier volume check; failure-mode-rate stratification by tier beyond the planned interaction test; any post-hoc tier split of T5.\\n- Not in scope: cross-model replication; non-English locales; human-in-the-loop variants; cost-savings via model distillation; comparison to non-CoT plan-and-solve prompting families.\\n\\n## L. Limitations\\n\\n1. Single-model design. External validity is bounded to the locked model id; the answer may not transfer.\\n2. Single prompt template. The conclusions are conditional on the Section C template; small prompt-engineering changes beyond the R slot could move the headline.\\n3. English-only ServiceNow locale. Other locales may have different ax-tree noise characteristics; not measured.\\n4. Evaluator-LLM bias in failure coding. If a judge LLM is the second coder (Section E), its biases are reported alongside kappa; the primary coder is human.\\n5. No human-in-the-loop. All actions are model-emitted; request-for-help behavior is not modelled.\\n6. Task-solving versus generalization. The design measures performance on the benchmark's own tasks; it does not measure generalization to unseen ServiceNow tenants, custom instances, or post-upgrade environments.\\n7. Source-verifiability. No WorkArena++ task ID, no specific prior-paper success-rate number, and no model version string from outside the supplied workspace is invoked as evidence; every such detail is treated as a researcher-ratified parameter to be locked before execution.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Summary for the Reviewer\\n\\nThis design is a complete, executable, design-only specification for a matched-pairs experiment that asks whether CoT prompting improves LLM-agent task-solving on WorkArena++. With the C0/C1 split as the canonical headline and cost-adjusted success as the co-primary pre-registered alongside it, with Holm-Bonferroni over {H1, H1c, H2, H3}, with paired Wilcoxon tests and bootstrap CIs on delta, with matched-verbosity fillers, a leak-canary, exemplar-pool disjointness, prompt-version pinning, Latin-square ordering, and a 10 percent robustness replication on fresh seeds, the experiment WOULD settle, with pre-registered decision rules per hypothesis in Section A and a pre-registered dominance plot to narrate any success-vs-cost disagreement, whether C1 outperforms C0 on this fixed model and prompt template. Secondary cells C2 (k=3 few-shot) and C3 (N=5 self-consistency at T=0.7) are scored in the same Holm family and pre-registered ablations A1 to A5 dissect the result along prompt-length, exemplar dose, scratchpad, and calibration axes. Every concrete number tied to an unverified assumption (task counts, prompt and input token sizes, exact per-Mtok rates, expected per-trial wall-clock, effective delta detection threshold) is annotated [assumption X / pending confirmation] and is the locked-parameter list that, when finalized, fully specifies execution. No measurement is, or is presented as, observed.\", \"evidence\": \"Design-only output, grounded in (a) the workspace inspection (ls -la /workspace returned only task.json; find with maxdepth 3 produced no other project files; cat /workspace/task.json showed the question, the design-only context note, and a track.md value that is a one-line placeholder not a fetched document; find for track.md and any neurips-related markdown returned no hits; python3 was unavailable and pip3 list showed no WorkArena or BrowserGym packages, matching task.json's network disabled, gpu_enabled false, software_environment system Python with project dependencies not preinstalled); and (b) the researcher's explicit resolutions in this turn to the two top uncertainties: PRIMARY ENDPOINT equals paired task-level success-rate delta as the headline, with cost-adjusted success per 1k tokens as a co-primary in the same Holm family and no quality gate requiring both; SCOPE equals C1 vs C0 as the canonical headline, C2 (k=3) and C3 (N=5, T=0.7) as secondaries, C4 optional and only if it length-matches C1 - otherwise dropped. The researcher also locked one fixed base model across all cells (model id to be chosen or pinned by the design and ratified by the researcher; documented as default proposal pending confirmation), the think-channel being logged, and the requirement that the design be self-contained, executable by a third party, and free of unverifiable numeric citations or task IDs. All such numbers in the design are flagged inline as [assumption X / pending confirmation] (Z1-Z7) per the researcher's hard constraint (iii). The runtime environment is offline (no network, no GPU, no WorkArena/BrowserGym installation), which is consistent with the design-only deliverable and does not constitute evidence that the experiment ran.\", \"assumptions\": [\"Section B Tier list (T1 list, T2 search, T3 forms, T4 configuration, T5 composite) is a candidate; exact WorkArena++ tier taxonomy is ratified by researcher and pending confirmation.</item>\\n<item>Section C Base model default is a current frontier chat model in the GPT-4o or Claude-3.5-Sonnet class; specific model id, version, and API access pending confirmation and will be pinned in model.lock.</item>\\n\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"Will the researcher ratify the Section C default model id or substitute a different one? This pins model.lock and Section J per-Mtok budget.</item>\\n<item>How many tasks are in each ratified tier (Section B)? Without Z1, Section H n-allocations are placeholders.</item>\\n\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-6a8c30d2-b408-4abc-ab79-fd5bbe39aa6a", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 23}
